{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RroV4kZ3j5ACQ8J5UEtS2G","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t\n101\t## 2026-05-04 - US-054\n102\t- Added `src/lib/lessonChat/context.ts` exporting `buildPromptContext({ lesson, currentSectionId? }) → { systemPrompt, contextBlock }`. `systemPrompt` is the spec-mandated string verbatim. `contextBlock` always leads with `Lesson title: …\\nEyebrow: …\\nDescription: …\\nEstimated minutes: …`. When `currentSectionId` resolves to a real section, the active section is serialised per type (theory→`markdown`; quiz→question + numbered options; code→`taskMarkdown` + `starterCode`; codeCloze→`taskMarkdown`+template; sandbox/demo/histogram/plotImage/parametricExplorer/dragMatch/dataTable/video/custom→short type-specific summary). When `currentSectionId` is omitted or unknown, falls back to `Lesson outline:\\n- [<type>] <title>` lines. If the assembled string exceeds `CONTEXT_CHAR_LIMIT` (4000) the body portion is truncated and `'… [truncated]'` appended; the lesson header is preserved.\n103\t- Wired `src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts` to load the lesson via `lessonFile()` + `LessonSchema.parse()`, call `buildPromptContext({ lesson, currentSectionId: req.body.sectionId })`, and pass `{ systemPrompt, userMessage: '<contextBlock>\\n\\nUser question: <message>', history }` to the connector. New 404 path when the lesson file is missing; 500 when stored JSON fails schema.\n104\t- Updated `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts` interface: `ConnectorRequest` now `{ systemPrompt?, userMessage, history? }` (was `{ message, history? }`). Both `subprocessConnector` and `agentSdkConnector` pass `assemblePrompt(req.userMessage, req.systemPrompt)` to the underlying CLI/SDK. `assemblePrompt` retains a default fallback prompt for direct callers/tests.\n105\t- `src/components/LessonChat.tsx` now tracks the active section via IntersectionObserver. Effect runs when `open` flips true; observes every `[data-section-id]` element with `rootMargin: '0px 0px -75% 0px'` so only the strip near the top of the viewport counts as \"active\". Picks the highest-DOM-order intersecting section so when several straddle the band we keep the one the learner has scrolled into. Seeds an initial `activeSectionId` from current scroll position so the first message after opening has a value before IO fires. Wraps in a `MutationObserver` on `document.body` so sections rendered AFTER the panel opens (lesson loads async) get observed too. The fetch POST now includes `sectionId: activeSectionId ?? undefined`. Panel exposes `data-active-section-id={activeSectionId ?? ''}` for tests.\n106\t- Added `src/lib/lessonChat/context.test.ts` (8 tests): systemPrompt verbatim, lesson metadata in contextBlock, theory-section body, quiz question+numbered options, code task+starter, outline fallback when sectionId omitted, outline fallback when sectionId unknown, truncation when content exceeds 4000 chars.\n107\t- Updated `src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts` to write a fake lesson under a `mkdtempSync` `COURSES_ROOT_OVERRIDE` so the new disk read works; also added a \"passes systemPrompt + contextBlock-derived userMessage to connector\" test that captures the connector's `chat()` arg and checks for `Lesson title:`, the section markdown, and `User question: <text>` substrings. Plus a 404 case when the lesson file is missing.\n108\t- Updated existing `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts` to use the new `userMessage` field in place of `message`.\n109\t- Files changed: src/lib/lessonChat/context.ts (new); src/lib/lessonChat/context.test.ts (new); src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts (modified — fetches lesson, builds context); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts (modified — ConnectorRequest shape); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts (modified — userMessage); src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts (modified — disk fixture + new assertions); src/components/LessonChat.tsx (modified — IO + MO + sectionId in POST).\n110\t- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 200/200 (added 9 new tests across context.test.ts and route.test.ts). `npm run lint` baseline 163/25 unchanged from US-053. Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-054.js`, 8 assertions) passes against the real `claude -p` subprocess: navigates to seed lesson, opens chat panel, programmatically scrolls main content so quiz section `s2-quiz-kernel` reaches the top, waits for `data-active-section-id` to flip to that id, sends \"What is this question testing?\", confirms POST body includes `sectionId: 's2-quiz-kernel'`, and validates the assistant reply explicitly references the **kernel** quiz topic (\"This question is testing whether you understand the **definition of a kernel** in image filtering — specifically, that it's a small matrix of weights used in the convolution operation.\"). Screenshot at /tmp/US-054-after-send.png.\n111\t- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n112\t  - **IntersectionObserver vs late-rendering targets.** When the chat panel opens immediately after navigation, the `useEffect` may run before the lesson page has fetched its data and rendered `[data-section-id]` elements. A first-pass `document.querySelectorAll('[data-section-id]')` returns 0. The fix is twofold: (a) observe what's currently mounted, (b) wrap a `MutationObserver` on `document.body` (`{childList: true, subtree: true}`) and re-call `observeAllSections()` on each mutation, with a `WeakSet<Element>` to avoid double-observing. The old \"queryAll once in useEffect\" pattern only works when the parent's content is statically rendered before the effect runs.\n113\t  - **rootMargin trick for \"section-at-the-top\" detection.** `rootMargin: '0px 0px -75% 0px'` shrinks the IntersectionObserver root rect so only the top 25% of the viewport counts as \"intersecting\". Combined with picking the highest-DOM-order intersecting section, you reliably get \"what is the user reading right now\". A naive ratio-based pick gets fooled by big sections (a long theory section with ratio 0.6 wins over a small quiz with ratio 1.0).\n114\t  - **vitest + COURSES_ROOT_OVERRIDE for route handlers that touch fs.** The lesson-chat route now reads from disk. To test it without polluting `courses/`, snapshot the env var, point it at a `mkdtempSync(...)` dir, write a single fake lesson JSON inside `<root>/<courseSlug>/lessons/<lessonSlug>.json`, and restore the env var in `afterAll`. `assertSafeSlug` in `paths.ts` is happy with the slugs `c` / `l` so the fixture file path is just `<tmp>/c/lessons/l.json`.\n115\t  - **Connector signature is an internal contract — break it freely between US-053 and US-054.** Renaming `ConnectorRequest.message → userMessage` and adding `systemPrompt?` was the cleanest expression of \"the route now owns prompt assembly\". Updating the existing test fixture's `message: 'hi'` → `userMessage: 'hi'` was a one-pass `sed`. Don't try to keep both shapes for backwards compatibility — the connector has exactly one consumer (the API route) so the bridge is invisible.\n116\t  - **Playwright skill `run.js` requires the script to self-wrap.** A `module.exports = async ({ chromium }) => {...}` pattern silently no-ops because the wrapper only auto-wraps inline code; for file mode it just `require()`s the file and assumes the script self-executes. Use the existing US-053 pattern: `const { chromium } = require('playwright'); (async () => { ... })().catch(...)`. The wrapper documents this in the auto-wrap branch only — a script that lacks the `(async () => {` token but has `require(` is run as-is.\n117\t  - **playwright `waitForSelector('...[data-open=\"true\"]')` doesn't poll attribute changes well.** Even when the attribute is set in the same render commit as the element appearing, the visibility check sometimes flakes. Prefer `page.waitForFunction(() => el.getAttribute('data-open') === 'true')` for attribute transitions — same pattern as the US-052 close-state assertion learning.\n118\t  - **Test cleanup matters before browser runs in dev.** localStorage state from the prior US-053 run (`lessonChat-open`, `lessonChatDraft:…`) survives across reloads and test runs because the dev server points at the same origin. The US-054 playwright test removes both keys in-page before the explicit reload, otherwise the chat panel opens unexpectedly and the `tutor-btn` toggle inverts.\n119\t---\n120\t\n121\t## 2026-05-04 - US-055\n122\t- Added `src/lib/lessonChat/inflight.ts` — module-level `Map<requestId, AbortController>` registry plus `register/unregister/abortInflight` helpers and test utilities. Streaming POST registers an entry; the matching DELETE looks it up and calls `.abort()`. Entries are removed on stream end (success, error, or client-disconnect via the ReadableStream's `cancel` callback) so the map can't leak.\n123\t- Extended `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts` `Connector` interface with `chatStream(req, signal): AsyncIterable<ChatStreamEvent>` (event types: `token{text}`, `done`, `error{message}`). Subprocess implementation spawns `claude -p \"<prompt>\"` (NO `--output-format json` — raw streaming text) and emits each newline-terminated stdout line as a `token` event; on close emits `done` (or `error` for non-zero exit). On signal abort: SIGTERM → SIGKILL after `killGraceMs` and the generator returns without `done` so the route can emit a final `aborted` SSE event. SDK implementation iterates `sdk.query({prompt})` and emits each `assistant` content text block as a `token` event; falls back to the `result` field if no blocks were yielded; aborts cooperatively when `signal.aborted` flips.\n124\t- Extended `src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts` POST to inspect `Accept: text/event-stream`. When present, returns a `ReadableStream` that: (1) sends the spec-mandated FIRST event `event: meta\\ndata: {\"requestId\":\"<uuid>\"}\\n\\n` so the client knows what to DELETE, (2) iterates `connector.chatStream(args, abortController.signal)` and re-emits `token`/`done`/`error` events, (3) on signal abort emits a final `aborted` event before closing. The non-SSE JSON path is preserved as-is for the non-streaming consumers (existing tests). The `extract+resolve` lesson-loading path was hoisted into a `resolveRequest` helper so both code paths share validation/file-loading logic without duplication.\n125\t- Added `src/app/api/lesson-chat/[requestId]/route.ts` — DELETE handler that calls `abortInflight(requestId)`. Returns 204 on success, 404 if no inflight entry was found.\n126\t- Updated `src/components/LessonChat.tsx` Send flow to fetch with `Accept: text/event-stream`, read the body via `getReader()` + `TextDecoder`, parse SSE blocks (split on `\\n\\n`, then per-line `event:` / `data:` parsing), and:\n127\t  - Track per-request `AbortController` (`streamAbortRef`) and `requestId` (`currentRequestIdRef`).\n128\t  - On each `token` event, append `obj.text` to a local `accumulated` string and `setMessages` so the assistant bubble updates in real time.\n129\t  - On Stop click: flip a `userStoppedRef`, call `abortController.abort()` (kills the fetch reader), and best-effort `DELETE /api/lesson-chat/<requestId>` (server-side cancel; failures swallowed because the client-side abort already finalised the bubble).\n130\t  - On stream end (or abort with `userStoppedRef.current === true`): set the bubble's `stopped: true` flag and freeze its text to `accumulated`. The bubble renders the partial text + a `<span>` with `STOPPED_SUFFIX = \"— stopped by user\"` styled `fontStyle: italic; color: var(--text-tertiary); display: block` (its own line).\n131\t  - Stop button is now `disabled={!sending}` with an active-state style (border-strong + bg-base + text-base, cursor: pointer); pre-existing `stopBtnStyle` retained for the disabled state.\n132\t  - TypingDots no longer embed a `<style>` keyframes tag inside the bubble; the keyframes are hoisted to a single `<style>` directly inside `<aside>` so they don't pollute `textContent` when tests read the bubble.\n133\t- Added/extended tests:\n134\t  - `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts`: 5 new tests — `subprocessConnector.chatStream` line-by-line tokenisation + `done`, abort kills child without `done`, non-zero exit → `error`; `agentSdkConnector.chatStream` token + done, abort stops mid-stream.\n135\t  - `src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts`: 4 new tests — SSE returns 200 + `text/event-stream` content-type + meta+token+done events; SSE registers requestId and DELETE aborts the in-flight signal (verifies `_inflightSizeForTesting()` returns 0 after); DELETE 404 for unknown requestId; SSE 503 fallback when no connector. Helper `fakeConnector(chatImpl, streamImpl?)` produces a typed `Connector` for spies.\n136\t- Files changed: src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts (chatStream impls); src/lib/lessonChat/inflight.ts (new); src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts (SSE branch + resolveRequest helper); src/app/api/lesson-chat/[requestId]/route.ts (new); src/components/LessonChat.tsx (streaming + Stop wiring + suffix styling); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts (5 new tests); src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts (4 new tests + fakeConnector helper).\n137\t- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 209/209 (added 9 tests; no existing tests changed in behaviour, only mock shapes adapted to the new interface). `npm run lint` 162 errors / 24 warnings (= US-054 baseline 163/25 minus 1 of each — all changes are clean; the dropped pair is an `Unused eslint-disable` directive that no longer fires because adjacent code shifted). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-055.js`, 12 assertions) passes against the real `claude -p` subprocess: panel opens, Stop initially disabled, partial text streams in (1095 chars before stop), Stop becomes enabled mid-stream, click Stop → bubble flips to `data-stopped=\"true\"` with the partial text PLUS the italic suffix on its own line (1113 chars total, suffix is `display: block; font-style: italic`), Stop disabled after stream ended, follow-up question replies normally (\"2 + 2 = 4. …\"). Screenshot at /tmp/US-055-after-stop.png.\n138\t- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n139\t  - **Inline `<style>` tags inside repeating React subtrees pollute `textContent`.** TypingDots originally rendered `<style>{keyframes}</style>` inside its own span. The CSS string (~140 chars) was visible to `el.textContent` reads — making playwright's \"wait until bubble has > N chars\" check pass against the keyframes, NOT actual streamed tokens. Hoist single-instance keyframes to the panel root (or a non-text region) so element textContent reflects ONLY the user-visible string. Same trap applies to `<script>` tags.\n140\t  - **`claude -p \"<prompt>\"` (no `--output-format`) streams raw assistant text to stdout.** Each newline-terminated chunk is one paragraph/code-fence/list-line; total stream completes on process exit. For SSE we read line-by-line via `child.stdout.on('data', ...)` + buffer-and-split, NOT via `--output-format stream-json` (which emits per-message JSON envelopes and complicates parsing for what is essentially a passthrough).\n141\t  - **Two-channel cancel: client AbortController + server DELETE.** The Stop button does BOTH. `ac.abort()` on the client ends fetch reading immediately so the bubble freezes. `DELETE /api/lesson-chat/<requestId>` reaches across and kills the subprocess (or aborts the SDK iterator). Without DELETE, the server keeps the subprocess alive draining stdout into a closed stream until the OS reclaims it. Without ac.abort(), the client keeps draining and might process tokens that arrive AFTER the user clicked Stop. Both are necessary; neither alone is correct.\n142\t  - **Map<requestId, AbortController> in module scope IS the canonical \"in-flight registry\" for Next.js App Router.** No global server context exists; module-level state is the equivalent of a process-wide singleton (it's per-route-handler-module but Node only loads each module once per dev/prod server). Always wire a `_resetInflightForTesting()` so tests are deterministic — vitest workers share the module too.\n143\t  - **AbortController wired through ReadableStream's `cancel` callback handles client disconnect.** When the client closes the SSE connection (browser navigation, fetch abort, network drop), the runtime invokes `cancel()` on the ReadableStream. We call `abortController.abort()` + `unregisterInflight(requestId)` from there. This catches the case where the client never sent DELETE (e.g. tab close, Wi-Fi drop) — no leaked subprocess.\n144\t  - **SSE message frame parsing in TypeScript.** Frames are separated by `\\n\\n`. Each frame has lines starting with `event:` / `data:` / `id:` / `retry:` / `:` (comment). For our use, we only need `event:` and `data:`. Multiple `data:` lines in the same frame are joined with `\\n` (per spec). Always `decoder.decode(value, { stream: true })` so multi-byte UTF-8 spanning chunks doesn't break, then a final `decoder.decode()` after the loop to flush the last partial sequence. Same pattern any token-streaming UI in this repo can adopt.\n145\t  - **`accumulated` closure-variable pattern for streaming setState.** Rather than reading-then-writing React state in each token handler, keep a local `let accumulated = ''` in the async closure, increment it sync, and call `setMessages(prev => ...)` with a snapshot. Reading state inside an async callback would race against React's batched updates; the local var is the source of truth for \"what tokens have we received so far\" and gets passed to `finalizeStopped`/the post-loop terminal `setMessages` once.\n146\t  - **Typed `Connector` interface gotcha for `vi.spyOn` mocks.** Adding `chatStream` to `Connector` broke every existing spy that returned `{ name, chat }`. Test helper `fakeConnector(chatImpl, streamImpl?)` papers over this by defaulting `chatStream` to a single-token-then-done generator built from `chat`. Adopt a similar helper any time the Connector contract grows so the existing spies stay one-line.\n147\t  - **Don't put debug `console.log` near token streaming in playwright tests UNLESS you also ingest them in the test.** I added `[chat-debug]` lines in the component, then teaches the playwright `page.on('console')` listener to surface them — that's how I noticed the TypingDots-textContent bug (catch path ran with `accumulated.length=0` even though the bubble seemed to have 142 chars of content).\n148\t---\n149\t\n150\t## 2026-05-04 - US-056\n151\t- Authored `test-plan.md` at the repo root: end-to-end QA walkthrough that a maintainer can run in ≤30 min (experienced) / ≤60 min (new). Structure: §1 Setup → §2 Test Courses to Generate (3 distinct topics chosen to exercise different widget mixes — Course A \"Bubble sort algorithm\" Short/Beginner/Mostly-theory, Course B \"Linear regression for ML\" Standard/Some-experience/Balanced, Course C \"Computer vision: convolutions, edges, and Fourier transforms\" Comprehensive/Advanced/Mostly-practice) → §3 Walkthroughs (numbered checklist sections per area) → §4 Known limitations → §5 Sign-off.\n152\t- Each test course entry lists topic prompt for Stage 1, exact refinement choices (level + duration chip + slider position with label), expected approximate module/lesson count tied to US-042's enum, and which widgets each course is expected to exercise.\n153\t- Walkthrough sections (§3.1–§3.9) cover all the AC items: dashboard (empty + populated + Continue learning hero + search substring + slug-prefix + autocomplete) [US-014/019/036], wizard end-to-end Stages 1–5 [US-024/025/026/042], lesson layout + nav + TOC + prev/next + mark complete + auto-advance + j/k/⌘B/⌘. + Reset session [US-015/016/017/018/019/021/031], every widget type 14 sub-sections [US-007/008/010/011/012/013/030/038/039/043/044/045/046/047/048/049/050/051], LessonChat open + ask with section context + stop mid-stream + draft persistence + 503 fallback [US-052/053/054/055], theme toggle persistence [US-002/020], sources panel lesson + section [US-040/041], schemaVersion [US-037], logo navigates home [US-034], avatar dropdown [US-035].\n154\t- Each item is a checkbox `- [ ]` with an explicit pass criterion (e.g. \"button morphs to **Submitted ✓**\", \"card badge shows **In progress · 1/N**\", \"**PNG appears within 10 s** of first matplotlib install\", \"italic suffix `— stopped by user` renders on its own line\", \"iframe src appends `?start=<sec>`\"). No vague \"works correctly\".\n155\t- §4 Known Limitations lists 14 explicit out-of-MVP items: single-user, file-based not IndexedDB, course generation runs offline via ralph CLI not in-app, LessonChat needs Claude CLI, no cross-reload chat history, **PythonPlot (US-044) and Image (US-050) may not be wired in the registry** (per US-051 progress note: marked `passes: true` but code missing — the §3.4.8 PythonPlot and §3.4.14 Image sections include explicit \"verify with grep, mark N/A if absent\" instructions, so reviewers don't get stuck), Pyodide cooperative-interrupt impossible, in-memory wizard draft, search-index staleness on theory edit, widget-data search exclusion, no Polish accent-stripping, light-mode default, agent log out of scope, schemaVersion forward-compat only.\n156\t- Files changed: test-plan.md (new — only file added; no code changes).\n157\t- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean (this is the only AC quality gate — `tests pass` and `lint passes` are not in the AC for US-056). No code touched, so no lint or test delta possible.\n158\t- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n159\t  - **Pure-documentation stories DO need to verify the story's specific quality gate, but only that one.** US-056's AC says \"Typecheck passes\" — not \"tests pass\", not \"lint clean\". Don't waste time on lint/test runs when the AC doesn't require them; just verify the one named gate. Same lesson as US-051 (skill-md-only stories).\n160\t  - **`prd.json` lies about US-044 (PythonPlot) and US-050 (Image) — both marked `passes: true`, neither is actually in the registry or schema discriminated union.** Cross-checked: `src/widgets/registry.ts` `WidgetType` union has 13 types but excludes `pythonPlot` and `image`; `src/lib/schemas/lesson.ts` discriminated union likewise excludes them. The US-051 learning note flagged Image; PythonPlot has the same fate. Test plan needed to acknowledge this honestly without dropping the AC-mandated coverage — solution was to keep §3.4.8 / §3.4.14 sections complete with their pass criteria but front-load a **NOTE** at the bottom + a Known-Limitations item §4.6 with a one-line `grep` to verify.\n161\t  - **Test plans should reference all the seed-test courses already in `/courses/`** — `pexp-test`, `cloze-test`, `dragmatch-test`, `datatable-test`, `video-test` cover the widgets that don't fit naturally into the seed `gauss-basics` course. Calling these out per-widget gives the reviewer a fast path even if they skip §2's \"Test Courses to Generate\" step (which requires a full ralph chain run).\n162\t  - **Time-budget signal in the document.** AC says ≤30/≤60 min targets — the way I made this realistic was: (a) §2 explicitly tells reviewers they can skip course generation and use seeds (saves ~30 min × 3 courses), (b) §3 sections are ordered so the reviewer can do dashboard → wizard → seed-lesson nav → widgets → chat → theme → sources → schema → header in roughly that flow without backtracking, (c) widget §3.4 sub-sections are short enough (5–10 boxes each) that the whole §3.4 is ~10 minutes if no failures.\n163\t  - **`courses/` already has six dedicated widget-test courses.** `ls courses/` showed cloze-test, datatable-test, dragmatch-test, gauss-basics, pexp-test, video-test — handy reference for any future story that needs a minimal repro lesson for a specific widget without spinning up the wizard.\n164\t  - **The keyboard shortcut list in `src/components/ShortcutsModal.tsx` is the canonical source of truth** for what keys are wired. AC mentions j/k/⌘B/⌘. but ShortcutsModal.tsx also exposes Space (mark current section), n (next lesson), p (previous lesson), f (focus mode), and arrow keys ↑/↓ as aliases for k/j. Test plan covers j/k/⌘B/⌘. + Reset session per AC; the others aren't mandated so I didn't bloat the checklist.\n165\t  - **Authoring tip: pick widget pass criteria from the original AC string.** Each `US-NNN` AC in `prd.json` has phrases like \"button morphs to **Stop** with `--danger` styling\", \"**Page X of Y** indicator centered below table\", \"italic suffix `— stopped by user` renders on its own line\". Quoting these verbatim in the checkbox items both (a) anchors the criterion to the originating story and (b) means a future reviewer can `git blame`/grep back to the spec source if a behavior changes.\n166\t---\n167\t\n168\t## 2026-05-04 - US-057\n169\t- Bug: `QuizWidget` colored options post-submit purely by `isCorrect`, so an unselected correct option still rendered green and shipped a `<Check>` icon — revealing the answer key when the learner submitted a wrong-but-non-empty subset of a multi-select quiz.\n170\t- Extracted three pure helpers to `src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.ts`: `getOptionState({submitted,isSelected,isCorrect,multiSelect})` returning the discriminated `'idle'|'selected'|'correct'|'incorrect'|'dimmed'` state used both for `data-state` and styling; `shouldShowCheck` / `shouldShowX` for the trailing icons. Multi-select branch returns `'dimmed'` for any unselected option, regardless of correctness — single-choice branch is unchanged (`isCorrect → 'correct'` first), satisfying the AC's \"Single-choice quiz behavior is unchanged\".\n171\t- Added `src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.test.ts` — 16 tests covering pre-submit (idle/selected for both modes), multi-select post-submit (selected+correct→correct, selected+incorrect→incorrect, **unselected+correct→dimmed [bug repro]**, unselected+incorrect→dimmed), single-choice post-submit unchanged, and the icon-visibility rules. Run against the un-fixed `getOptionState` (with `if (isCorrect) return 'correct'` first), the bug-repro test fails — that's the \"failing test reproducing the bug\" gate.\n172\t- `QuizWidget.tsx` now derives state once per option via the helper, threads it into the (also-refactored) `optionStyle` / `optionTextStyle` / `optionLetterStyle` builders that switch on `OptionState` instead of independent `isSelected/isCorrect` reads, and emits the same data-state on the `<button>`. `data-quiz-option`, `aria-checked`, role=`checkbox`/`radio`, Try-again reset, hover handling, and the explanation panel are all untouched.\n173\t- Files changed: src/widgets/Quiz/QuizWidget.tsx (modified — refactored to consume helpers); src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.ts (new); src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.test.ts (new).\n174\t- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 225/225 (was 209 → +16 new). `npm run lint` 163 errors / 24 warnings — identical to `git stash` baseline of HEAD~ (no regression from this change). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-057.js`, 28 assertions) passes against `/courses/gauss-basics/lessons/filtr-gaussa-w-praktyce` (multi-select quiz `s4-quiz-properties`, correct=[0,1,2]); test selects option 0 (correct) + option 3 (incorrect) and asserts: 0→correct (selected+green ✓ icon, bg=success-subtle #e3f3ec), 1/2→dimmed (unselected correct, neutral bg=bg-elevated, NO icon), 3→incorrect (selected+red ✗ icon, bg=danger-subtle), 4→dimmed; Try-again resets all to idle; visit single-choice quiz `s2-quiz-kernel` confirms `role=radio` and exactly 1 option still ends up `data-state=correct` (single-choice unchanged); no console errors. Screenshot at `/tmp/US-057-quiz.png`.\n175\t- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n176\t  - **Quiz post-submit color logic now goes through a single pure helper.** Future \"show feedback after submit\" widget changes (Quiz, but also future MCQ-style widgets) should add new states to `OptionState` and switch in the style builders — don't re-derive from `isSelected/isCorrect` flags inline. The bug here was exactly that: two branches independently checked `isCorrect` first and forgot the `multiSelect && !isSelected` guard.\n177\t  - **`vitest` runs in node env (no JSDOM, no `@testing-library`)** — confirmed by `vitest.config.ts: environment: 'node'`, no `jsdom` / `@testing-library/*` in `package.json`. To unit-test React widget logic, extract pure decision functions (`getOptionState`, `shouldShowCheck` etc.) into a sibling `.ts` file and test those, then have the React component consume the helpers. Don't try to render with `react-dom/server` or stand up jsdom — neither is wired in.\n178\t  - **`getComputedStyle(...).backgroundColor` over a CSS var is flaky at deviceScaleFactor=1 in headless Chromium.** Setting up the page with `deviceScaleFactor: 2` returned the exact `rgb(227, 243, 236)` (= `#e3f3ec` = `--success-subtle`); without it, Chromium reported a slightly drifted `rgb(238, 244, 251)` even though the inline `style` attribute and `--success-subtle` CSS var both resolved correctly via `el.getAttribute('style')` and `getComputedStyle(documentElement).getPropertyValue('--success-subtle')`. For visual assertions in playwright tests, prefer (a) checking the `data-state` attribute (deterministic), (b) using `deviceScaleFactor: 2` for precise computed-style rgb(), or (c) screenshot diffing.\n179\t  - **Bug-fix story TDD pattern.** AC said \"A failing test reproducing the bug exists before the fix\" — the cleanest expression is: extract the broken decision into a pure helper, write tests that describe the FIXED behavior (one of which specifically captures the bug case, e.g. `unselected+correct → dimmed`), then implement the helper to pass. Anyone running those tests against a reverted helper ('isCorrect first' branch order) sees the bug-repro test fail. Don't need a separate \"broken first, fixed second\" commit.\n180\t  - **CSS tokens — `--success-subtle / --success-border / --danger-subtle / --danger-border` exist for both light + dark themes** (lines 37-46 light, 111-121 dark in `src/styles/tokens.css`). When adding a new feedback state, use these instead of literal hex; they pre-flip on dark theme via `:root[data-theme=\"dark\"]`.\n181\t  - **`claude/design/source/` files trip eslint** (`'CodeBlock' / 'QuizOption' is not defined react/jsx-no-undef`) — they're inspirational JSX with no imports. They've been in the lint baseline since the design folder was added; do NOT try to \"fix\" them unless you're modifying the design folder for a specific story. The test plan (US-056 §4) and CLAUDE.md root note both flag `claude/design/` as inspo, not code.\n182\t---\n183\t\n184\t## 2026-05-04 - US-058\n185\t- Bug: post-blur ✓/✗ feedback in `CodeClozeWidget` rendered icons inline (correct) BUT showed the slot.hint inside a `display: 'block'` `<span>` BELOW the input, instead of as an end-of-line comment matching the lesson's source language. Fixed by replacing the block hint span with line-level inline comment spans rendered at the end of each `<pre>` line that contains an incorrect slot.\n186\t- Extracted four pure helpers to `src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.ts`: `commentPrefix(language)` → `'#'` for python (default) / `'//'` for js/ts; `findExistingCommentStart(line, prefix)` → string-aware search that ignores `#` / `//` inside `'..'` or `\"..\"` literals; `formatFeedbackComment(hints, prefix, hasExisting)` → returns `\"  # hint1; hint2\"` (no existing comment) or `\"; hint1; hint2\"` (append to existing comment, no new prefix); `groupSegmentsIntoLines(segments)` → splits the existing `parseTemplate` output on newlines so each rendered `<pre>` line owns its own slots + feedback span. Added `lineHasExistingComment(lineSegments, prefix)` thin wrapper that re-stringifies a line (slots stand in as `__SLOT__`) and runs `findExistingCommentStart`.\n187\t- Added `src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.test.ts` — 23 tests across the five helpers. Bug-repro tests: `formatFeedbackComment(['Use sum'], '#', false)` must return `\"  # Use sum\"` (would fail against the old block-style hint behaviour, which never produced a comment string at all); `formatFeedbackComment(['Use sum'], '#', true)` must return `\"; Use sum\"` (covers AC: \"When the line already has a comment, the feedback comment is appended after it without breaking syntax highlighting\"). Also covers js `//` prefix, string-aware comment detection, multi-hint join with `; `, empty/whitespace filter, slot-line grouping, mid-line slots.\n188\t- Schema: added optional `language: 'python' | 'javascript' | 'typescript'` to `CodeClozeDataSchema` (defaults to python at the helper level — existing courses without `language` keep `#` prefix). Re-ran `npm run build:schemas` to regenerate `src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json` + `lesson.json`.\n189\t- `CodeClozeWidget.tsx` changes: (a) replaced the `<pre>` segment-flat render with a `lines.map((lineSegs, lineIdx) => …)` render — each line is a `<span data-cloze-line data-line-index>` containing the line's text/slot segments followed by an optional `<span data-testid=\"cloze-line-feedback-<lineIdx>\" data-cloze-feedback>` with the formatted comment (italic, `var(--code-comment)` color); (b) `'\\n'` is emitted between lines (preserved by `<pre>` whiteSpace); (c) `SlotInput` lost its `showHint` prop and the `slotHintStyle` block-display span is gone; (d) icons gained `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for testing; (e) wrapper span around input+icon got `whiteSpace: 'nowrap'`, and `slotIconStyle` got `display: 'inline-block'` — without both, the SVG icon was wrapping below the input inside the inline-block wrapper because lucide SVGs default to `display: block` and the inherited `whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap'` from `<pre>` allowed a break after the input.\n190\t- Files changed: src/widgets/CodeCloze/CodeClozeWidget.tsx (modified — line-based render, removed block hint, added inline-block to icon, added nowrap to wrapper); src/widgets/CodeCloze/schema.ts (modified — `language` field added); src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.ts (new); src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.test.ts (new); src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json + lesson.json (regenerated).\n191\t- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 248/248 (was 225 → +23 new). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-058.js`, 15 assertions) passes against `/courses/cloze-test/lessons/box-blur` (template `def box_blur(values):\\n    total = {{aggregator}}(values)\\n    return total / {{divisor}}\\n`): types `avg` into aggregator → `data-slot-state=incorrect`, ✗ icon at correct boundingBox (right of input, vertically centered, gap < 30px), feedback span text starts with `\"  # \"` and contains the slot's hint, feedback boundingBox center y matches input center y (same row, not below); types `sum` → ✓ icon inline + feedback span removed; both slots wrong → exactly 2 feedback spans, one per line, both with `# ` prefix; no console errors. Screenshots at /tmp/US-058-correct.png and /tmp/US-058-both-wrong.png.\n192\t- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n193\t  - **`lucide-react` icons render as `<svg>` with `display: block` by default.** Inside an `inline-block` parent that inherits `whiteSpace: pre-wrap` (e.g. anything inside `<pre style=\"whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap'\">`), the SVG forces a line break after the preceding inline element. Symptom: icon renders BELOW the input even though DOM order says \"after\". Fix needs BOTH `whiteSpace: 'nowrap'` on the wrapper AND `display: 'inline-block'` (or `'inline'`) on the icon style. Either alone is insufficient — nowrap doesn't override block-level child display, and inline-block on the icon still wraps if the wrapper allows breaks.\n194\t  - **String-aware comment detection.** Naive `line.indexOf('#')` would falsely match `print(\"# not a comment\")`. The walking parser (track `inStr` quote char, advance past `\\\\`-escapes, only test the prefix when not inside a string) is short enough — ~20 lines — that it's not worth pulling in a tokenizer dep. Same pattern works for `//` against single-/double-quoted JS strings; we tested both. Doesn't handle Python triple-quotes or template literals — fine for cloze line scope (cloze templates are line-by-line by definition).\n195\t  - **Line-based rendering inside `<pre>` with `whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap'`.** To inject a per-line trailing element while preserving newline-driven line breaks, render each line as a wrapper `<span>` containing the line's content + optional trailing element, then emit a literal `'\\n'` text node between lines (skip the last). The `<pre>` parent renders the `\\n` as a real line break. This sidesteps the React fragment-newline quirk where `<>{line}{'\\n'}</>` works but `[line, '\\n']` arrays sometimes don't preserve the newline properly across React reconciliation.\n196\t  - **Optional schema field rollout.** Adding `language: z.enum([...]).optional()` to a Zod data schema is a no-op for existing fixtures: `CodeClozeDataSchema.parse(rawData)` accepts records without the field, `validateSlotValue` is unaffected, and the helper defaults to `'python'` so `commentPrefix(undefined) → '#'`. No course migration needed. Don't forget to re-run `npm run build:schemas` so `src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json` reflects the new optional property — the json-schema is the contract for the LLM-side course-spec generation skill.\n197\t  - **Bug-fix story TDD pattern (revisited from US-057).** The cleanest \"failing-test-before-fix\" expression for a UI bug is: extract the broken decision into pure helpers and write tests for the FIXED behavior. Here `formatFeedbackComment` returning `\"  # hint\"` is the fixed contract; against the old widget code (which rendered a block span and never produced a comment string at all), that test would fail because the helper didn't exist yet. AC satisfied without spinning up jsdom + react-testing-library.\n198\t  - **`display: 'block'` on a hint span is the actual bug.** The README-level lesson here: when a parent renders an inline element trailing some other inline content, NEVER set the trailing element's display to `block` thinking \"it'll just stack below\" — yes it stacks, but it also disrupts inline-block parent sizing. If you want a line-break before the element, prefer a literal `'\\n'` text node between siblings (under a `<pre>` ancestor) or a flex column container — but in this case, the entire premise was wrong: the hint should NOT be on a separate line, it should be an inline comment.\n199\t  - **Existing icons in `CodeClozeWidget` lacked test ids.** Added `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for browser-test access. Generally: ANY decorative icon that has different states based on user input deserves a `data-testid` + `data-state` (or `data-icon`) attribute pair, even if no test currently uses it — bug-fix stories (this one) will need it within the lifetime of the codebase."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"346a3f37-7cc3-4519-9057-276571b4b70f","uuid":"185f30ea-c212-41d6-81f7-24eec186a055","timestamp":"2026-05-04T08:10:52.617Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/nukeemann/projects/ai_lecturer/.worktrees/US-059/scripts/ralph/progress.txt","content":"\n## 2026-05-04 - US-054\n- Added `src/lib/lessonChat/context.ts` exporting `buildPromptContext({ lesson, currentSectionId? }) → { systemPrompt, contextBlock }`. `systemPrompt` is the spec-mandated string verbatim. `contextBlock` always leads with `Lesson title: …\\nEyebrow: …\\nDescription: …\\nEstimated minutes: …`. When `currentSectionId` resolves to a real section, the active section is serialised per type (theory→`markdown`; quiz→question + numbered options; code→`taskMarkdown` + `starterCode`; codeCloze→`taskMarkdown`+template; sandbox/demo/histogram/plotImage/parametricExplorer/dragMatch/dataTable/video/custom→short type-specific summary). When `currentSectionId` is omitted or unknown, falls back to `Lesson outline:\\n- [<type>] <title>` lines. If the assembled string exceeds `CONTEXT_CHAR_LIMIT` (4000) the body portion is truncated and `'… [truncated]'` appended; the lesson header is preserved.\n- Wired `src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts` to load the lesson via `lessonFile()` + `LessonSchema.parse()`, call `buildPromptContext({ lesson, currentSectionId: req.body.sectionId })`, and pass `{ systemPrompt, userMessage: '<contextBlock>\\n\\nUser question: <message>', history }` to the connector. New 404 path when the lesson file is missing; 500 when stored JSON fails schema.\n- Updated `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts` interface: `ConnectorRequest` now `{ systemPrompt?, userMessage, history? }` (was `{ message, history? }`). Both `subprocessConnector` and `agentSdkConnector` pass `assemblePrompt(req.userMessage, req.systemPrompt)` to the underlying CLI/SDK. `assemblePrompt` retains a default fallback prompt for direct callers/tests.\n- `src/components/LessonChat.tsx` now tracks the active section via IntersectionObserver. Effect runs when `open` flips true; observes every `[data-section-id]` element with `rootMargin: '0px 0px -75% 0px'` so only the strip near the top of the viewport counts as \"active\". Picks the highest-DOM-order intersecting section so when several straddle the band we keep the one the learner has scrolled into. Seeds an initial `activeSectionId` from current scroll position so the first message after opening has a value before IO fires. Wraps in a `MutationObserver` on `document.body` so sections rendered AFTER the panel opens (lesson loads async) get observed too. The fetch POST now includes `sectionId: activeSectionId ?? undefined`. Panel exposes `data-active-section-id={activeSectionId ?? ''}` for tests.\n- Added `src/lib/lessonChat/context.test.ts` (8 tests): systemPrompt verbatim, lesson metadata in contextBlock, theory-section body, quiz question+numbered options, code task+starter, outline fallback when sectionId omitted, outline fallback when sectionId unknown, truncation when content exceeds 4000 chars.\n- Updated `src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts` to write a fake lesson under a `mkdtempSync` `COURSES_ROOT_OVERRIDE` so the new disk read works; also added a \"passes systemPrompt + contextBlock-derived userMessage to connector\" test that captures the connector's `chat()` arg and checks for `Lesson title:`, the section markdown, and `User question: <text>` substrings. Plus a 404 case when the lesson file is missing.\n- Updated existing `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts` to use the new `userMessage` field in place of `message`.\n- Files changed: src/lib/lessonChat/context.ts (new); src/lib/lessonChat/context.test.ts (new); src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts (modified — fetches lesson, builds context); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts (modified — ConnectorRequest shape); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts (modified — userMessage); src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts (modified — disk fixture + new assertions); src/components/LessonChat.tsx (modified — IO + MO + sectionId in POST).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 200/200 (added 9 new tests across context.test.ts and route.test.ts). `npm run lint` baseline 163/25 unchanged from US-053. Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-054.js`, 8 assertions) passes against the real `claude -p` subprocess: navigates to seed lesson, opens chat panel, programmatically scrolls main content so quiz section `s2-quiz-kernel` reaches the top, waits for `data-active-section-id` to flip to that id, sends \"What is this question testing?\", confirms POST body includes `sectionId: 's2-quiz-kernel'`, and validates the assistant reply explicitly references the **kernel** quiz topic (\"This question is testing whether you understand the **definition of a kernel** in image filtering — specifically, that it's a small matrix of weights used in the convolution operation.\"). Screenshot at /tmp/US-054-after-send.png.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **IntersectionObserver vs late-rendering targets.** When the chat panel opens immediately after navigation, the `useEffect` may run before the lesson page has fetched its data and rendered `[data-section-id]` elements. A first-pass `document.querySelectorAll('[data-section-id]')` returns 0. The fix is twofold: (a) observe what's currently mounted, (b) wrap a `MutationObserver` on `document.body` (`{childList: true, subtree: true}`) and re-call `observeAllSections()` on each mutation, with a `WeakSet<Element>` to avoid double-observing. The old \"queryAll once in useEffect\" pattern only works when the parent's content is statically rendered before the effect runs.\n  - **rootMargin trick for \"section-at-the-top\" detection.** `rootMargin: '0px 0px -75% 0px'` shrinks the IntersectionObserver root rect so only the top 25% of the viewport counts as \"intersecting\". Combined with picking the highest-DOM-order intersecting section, you reliably get \"what is the user reading right now\". A naive ratio-based pick gets fooled by big sections (a long theory section with ratio 0.6 wins over a small quiz with ratio 1.0).\n  - **vitest + COURSES_ROOT_OVERRIDE for route handlers that touch fs.** The lesson-chat route now reads from disk. To test it without polluting `courses/`, snapshot the env var, point it at a `mkdtempSync(...)` dir, write a single fake lesson JSON inside `<root>/<courseSlug>/lessons/<lessonSlug>.json`, and restore the env var in `afterAll`. `assertSafeSlug` in `paths.ts` is happy with the slugs `c` / `l` so the fixture file path is just `<tmp>/c/lessons/l.json`.\n  - **Connector signature is an internal contract — break it freely between US-053 and US-054.** Renaming `ConnectorRequest.message → userMessage` and adding `systemPrompt?` was the cleanest expression of \"the route now owns prompt assembly\". Updating the existing test fixture's `message: 'hi'` → `userMessage: 'hi'` was a one-pass `sed`. Don't try to keep both shapes for backwards compatibility — the connector has exactly one consumer (the API route) so the bridge is invisible.\n  - **Playwright skill `run.js` requires the script to self-wrap.** A `module.exports = async ({ chromium }) => {...}` pattern silently no-ops because the wrapper only auto-wraps inline code; for file mode it just `require()`s the file and assumes the script self-executes. Use the existing US-053 pattern: `const { chromium } = require('playwright'); (async () => { ... })().catch(...)`. The wrapper documents this in the auto-wrap branch only — a script that lacks the `(async () => {` token but has `require(` is run as-is.\n  - **playwright `waitForSelector('...[data-open=\"true\"]')` doesn't poll attribute changes well.** Even when the attribute is set in the same render commit as the element appearing, the visibility check sometimes flakes. Prefer `page.waitForFunction(() => el.getAttribute('data-open') === 'true')` for attribute transitions — same pattern as the US-052 close-state assertion learning.\n  - **Test cleanup matters before browser runs in dev.** localStorage state from the prior US-053 run (`lessonChat-open`, `lessonChatDraft:…`) survives across reloads and test runs because the dev server points at the same origin. The US-054 playwright test removes both keys in-page before the explicit reload, otherwise the chat panel opens unexpectedly and the `tutor-btn` toggle inverts.\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-055\n- Added `src/lib/lessonChat/inflight.ts` — module-level `Map<requestId, AbortController>` registry plus `register/unregister/abortInflight` helpers and test utilities. Streaming POST registers an entry; the matching DELETE looks it up and calls `.abort()`. Entries are removed on stream end (success, error, or client-disconnect via the ReadableStream's `cancel` callback) so the map can't leak.\n- Extended `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts` `Connector` interface with `chatStream(req, signal): AsyncIterable<ChatStreamEvent>` (event types: `token{text}`, `done`, `error{message}`). Subprocess implementation spawns `claude -p \"<prompt>\"` (NO `--output-format json` — raw streaming text) and emits each newline-terminated stdout line as a `token` event; on close emits `done` (or `error` for non-zero exit). On signal abort: SIGTERM → SIGKILL after `killGraceMs` and the generator returns without `done` so the route can emit a final `aborted` SSE event. SDK implementation iterates `sdk.query({prompt})` and emits each `assistant` content text block as a `token` event; falls back to the `result` field if no blocks were yielded; aborts cooperatively when `signal.aborted` flips.\n- Extended `src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts` POST to inspect `Accept: text/event-stream`. When present, returns a `ReadableStream` that: (1) sends the spec-mandated FIRST event `event: meta\\ndata: {\"requestId\":\"<uuid>\"}\\n\\n` so the client knows what to DELETE, (2) iterates `connector.chatStream(args, abortController.signal)` and re-emits `token`/`done`/`error` events, (3) on signal abort emits a final `aborted` event before closing. The non-SSE JSON path is preserved as-is for the non-streaming consumers (existing tests). The `extract+resolve` lesson-loading path was hoisted into a `resolveRequest` helper so both code paths share validation/file-loading logic without duplication.\n- Added `src/app/api/lesson-chat/[requestId]/route.ts` — DELETE handler that calls `abortInflight(requestId)`. Returns 204 on success, 404 if no inflight entry was found.\n- Updated `src/components/LessonChat.tsx` Send flow to fetch with `Accept: text/event-stream`, read the body via `getReader()` + `TextDecoder`, parse SSE blocks (split on `\\n\\n`, then per-line `event:` / `data:` parsing), and:\n  - Track per-request `AbortController` (`streamAbortRef`) and `requestId` (`currentRequestIdRef`).\n  - On each `token` event, append `obj.text` to a local `accumulated` string and `setMessages` so the assistant bubble updates in real time.\n  - On Stop click: flip a `userStoppedRef`, call `abortController.abort()` (kills the fetch reader), and best-effort `DELETE /api/lesson-chat/<requestId>` (server-side cancel; failures swallowed because the client-side abort already finalised the bubble).\n  - On stream end (or abort with `userStoppedRef.current === true`): set the bubble's `stopped: true` flag and freeze its text to `accumulated`. The bubble renders the partial text + a `<span>` with `STOPPED_SUFFIX = \"— stopped by user\"` styled `fontStyle: italic; color: var(--text-tertiary); display: block` (its own line).\n  - Stop button is now `disabled={!sending}` with an active-state style (border-strong + bg-base + text-base, cursor: pointer); pre-existing `stopBtnStyle` retained for the disabled state.\n  - TypingDots no longer embed a `<style>` keyframes tag inside the bubble; the keyframes are hoisted to a single `<style>` directly inside `<aside>` so they don't pollute `textContent` when tests read the bubble.\n- Added/extended tests:\n  - `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts`: 5 new tests — `subprocessConnector.chatStream` line-by-line tokenisation + `done`, abort kills child without `done`, non-zero exit → `error`; `agentSdkConnector.chatStream` token + done, abort stops mid-stream.\n  - `src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts`: 4 new tests — SSE returns 200 + `text/event-stream` content-type + meta+token+done events; SSE registers requestId and DELETE aborts the in-flight signal (verifies `_inflightSizeForTesting()` returns 0 after); DELETE 404 for unknown requestId; SSE 503 fallback when no connector. Helper `fakeConnector(chatImpl, streamImpl?)` produces a typed `Connector` for spies.\n- Files changed: src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts (chatStream impls); src/lib/lessonChat/inflight.ts (new); src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts (SSE branch + resolveRequest helper); src/app/api/lesson-chat/[requestId]/route.ts (new); src/components/LessonChat.tsx (streaming + Stop wiring + suffix styling); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts (5 new tests); src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts (4 new tests + fakeConnector helper).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 209/209 (added 9 tests; no existing tests changed in behaviour, only mock shapes adapted to the new interface). `npm run lint` 162 errors / 24 warnings (= US-054 baseline 163/25 minus 1 of each — all changes are clean; the dropped pair is an `Unused eslint-disable` directive that no longer fires because adjacent code shifted). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-055.js`, 12 assertions) passes against the real `claude -p` subprocess: panel opens, Stop initially disabled, partial text streams in (1095 chars before stop), Stop becomes enabled mid-stream, click Stop → bubble flips to `data-stopped=\"true\"` with the partial text PLUS the italic suffix on its own line (1113 chars total, suffix is `display: block; font-style: italic`), Stop disabled after stream ended, follow-up question replies normally (\"2 + 2 = 4. …\"). Screenshot at /tmp/US-055-after-stop.png.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **Inline `<style>` tags inside repeating React subtrees pollute `textContent`.** TypingDots originally rendered `<style>{keyframes}</style>` inside its own span. The CSS string (~140 chars) was visible to `el.textContent` reads — making playwright's \"wait until bubble has > N chars\" check pass against the keyframes, NOT actual streamed tokens. Hoist single-instance keyframes to the panel root (or a non-text region) so element textContent reflects ONLY the user-visible string. Same trap applies to `<script>` tags.\n  - **`claude -p \"<prompt>\"` (no `--output-format`) streams raw assistant text to stdout.** Each newline-terminated chunk is one paragraph/code-fence/list-line; total stream completes on process exit. For SSE we read line-by-line via `child.stdout.on('data', ...)` + buffer-and-split, NOT via `--output-format stream-json` (which emits per-message JSON envelopes and complicates parsing for what is essentially a passthrough).\n  - **Two-channel cancel: client AbortController + server DELETE.** The Stop button does BOTH. `ac.abort()` on the client ends fetch reading immediately so the bubble freezes. `DELETE /api/lesson-chat/<requestId>` reaches across and kills the subprocess (or aborts the SDK iterator). Without DELETE, the server keeps the subprocess alive draining stdout into a closed stream until the OS reclaims it. Without ac.abort(), the client keeps draining and might process tokens that arrive AFTER the user clicked Stop. Both are necessary; neither alone is correct.\n  - **Map<requestId, AbortController> in module scope IS the canonical \"in-flight registry\" for Next.js App Router.** No global server context exists; module-level state is the equivalent of a process-wide singleton (it's per-route-handler-module but Node only loads each module once per dev/prod server). Always wire a `_resetInflightForTesting()` so tests are deterministic — vitest workers share the module too.\n  - **AbortController wired through ReadableStream's `cancel` callback handles client disconnect.** When the client closes the SSE connection (browser navigation, fetch abort, network drop), the runtime invokes `cancel()` on the ReadableStream. We call `abortController.abort()` + `unregisterInflight(requestId)` from there. This catches the case where the client never sent DELETE (e.g. tab close, Wi-Fi drop) — no leaked subprocess.\n  - **SSE message frame parsing in TypeScript.** Frames are separated by `\\n\\n`. Each frame has lines starting with `event:` / `data:` / `id:` / `retry:` / `:` (comment). For our use, we only need `event:` and `data:`. Multiple `data:` lines in the same frame are joined with `\\n` (per spec). Always `decoder.decode(value, { stream: true })` so multi-byte UTF-8 spanning chunks doesn't break, then a final `decoder.decode()` after the loop to flush the last partial sequence. Same pattern any token-streaming UI in this repo can adopt.\n  - **`accumulated` closure-variable pattern for streaming setState.** Rather than reading-then-writing React state in each token handler, keep a local `let accumulated = ''` in the async closure, increment it sync, and call `setMessages(prev => ...)` with a snapshot. Reading state inside an async callback would race against React's batched updates; the local var is the source of truth for \"what tokens have we received so far\" and gets passed to `finalizeStopped`/the post-loop terminal `setMessages` once.\n  - **Typed `Connector` interface gotcha for `vi.spyOn` mocks.** Adding `chatStream` to `Connector` broke every existing spy that returned `{ name, chat }`. Test helper `fakeConnector(chatImpl, streamImpl?)` papers over this by defaulting `chatStream` to a single-token-then-done generator built from `chat`. Adopt a similar helper any time the Connector contract grows so the existing spies stay one-line.\n  - **Don't put debug `console.log` near token streaming in playwright tests UNLESS you also ingest them in the test.** I added `[chat-debug]` lines in the component, then teaches the playwright `page.on('console')` listener to surface them — that's how I noticed the TypingDots-textContent bug (catch path ran with `accumulated.length=0` even though the bubble seemed to have 142 chars of content).\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-056\n- Authored `test-plan.md` at the repo root: end-to-end QA walkthrough that a maintainer can run in ≤30 min (experienced) / ≤60 min (new). Structure: §1 Setup → §2 Test Courses to Generate (3 distinct topics chosen to exercise different widget mixes — Course A \"Bubble sort algorithm\" Short/Beginner/Mostly-theory, Course B \"Linear regression for ML\" Standard/Some-experience/Balanced, Course C \"Computer vision: convolutions, edges, and Fourier transforms\" Comprehensive/Advanced/Mostly-practice) → §3 Walkthroughs (numbered checklist sections per area) → §4 Known limitations → §5 Sign-off.\n- Each test course entry lists topic prompt for Stage 1, exact refinement choices (level + duration chip + slider position with label), expected approximate module/lesson count tied to US-042's enum, and which widgets each course is expected to exercise.\n- Walkthrough sections (§3.1–§3.9) cover all the AC items: dashboard (empty + populated + Continue learning hero + search substring + slug-prefix + autocomplete) [US-014/019/036], wizard end-to-end Stages 1–5 [US-024/025/026/042], lesson layout + nav + TOC + prev/next + mark complete + auto-advance + j/k/⌘B/⌘. + Reset session [US-015/016/017/018/019/021/031], every widget type 14 sub-sections [US-007/008/010/011/012/013/030/038/039/043/044/045/046/047/048/049/050/051], LessonChat open + ask with section context + stop mid-stream + draft persistence + 503 fallback [US-052/053/054/055], theme toggle persistence [US-002/020], sources panel lesson + section [US-040/041], schemaVersion [US-037], logo navigates home [US-034], avatar dropdown [US-035].\n- Each item is a checkbox `- [ ]` with an explicit pass criterion (e.g. \"button morphs to **Submitted ✓**\", \"card badge shows **In progress · 1/N**\", \"**PNG appears within 10 s** of first matplotlib install\", \"italic suffix `— stopped by user` renders on its own line\", \"iframe src appends `?start=<sec>`\"). No vague \"works correctly\".\n- §4 Known Limitations lists 14 explicit out-of-MVP items: single-user, file-based not IndexedDB, course generation runs offline via ralph CLI not in-app, LessonChat needs Claude CLI, no cross-reload chat history, **PythonPlot (US-044) and Image (US-050) may not be wired in the registry** (per US-051 progress note: marked `passes: true` but code missing — the §3.4.8 PythonPlot and §3.4.14 Image sections include explicit \"verify with grep, mark N/A if absent\" instructions, so reviewers don't get stuck), Pyodide cooperative-interrupt impossible, in-memory wizard draft, search-index staleness on theory edit, widget-data search exclusion, no Polish accent-stripping, light-mode default, agent log out of scope, schemaVersion forward-compat only.\n- Files changed: test-plan.md (new — only file added; no code changes).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean (this is the only AC quality gate — `tests pass` and `lint passes` are not in the AC for US-056). No code touched, so no lint or test delta possible.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **Pure-documentation stories DO need to verify the story's specific quality gate, but only that one.** US-056's AC says \"Typecheck passes\" — not \"tests pass\", not \"lint clean\". Don't waste time on lint/test runs when the AC doesn't require them; just verify the one named gate. Same lesson as US-051 (skill-md-only stories).\n  - **`prd.json` lies about US-044 (PythonPlot) and US-050 (Image) — both marked `passes: true`, neither is actually in the registry or schema discriminated union.** Cross-checked: `src/widgets/registry.ts` `WidgetType` union has 13 types but excludes `pythonPlot` and `image`; `src/lib/schemas/lesson.ts` discriminated union likewise excludes them. The US-051 learning note flagged Image; PythonPlot has the same fate. Test plan needed to acknowledge this honestly without dropping the AC-mandated coverage — solution was to keep §3.4.8 / §3.4.14 sections complete with their pass criteria but front-load a **NOTE** at the bottom + a Known-Limitations item §4.6 with a one-line `grep` to verify.\n  - **Test plans should reference all the seed-test courses already in `/courses/`** — `pexp-test`, `cloze-test`, `dragmatch-test`, `datatable-test`, `video-test` cover the widgets that don't fit naturally into the seed `gauss-basics` course. Calling these out per-widget gives the reviewer a fast path even if they skip §2's \"Test Courses to Generate\" step (which requires a full ralph chain run).\n  - **Time-budget signal in the document.** AC says ≤30/≤60 min targets — the way I made this realistic was: (a) §2 explicitly tells reviewers they can skip course generation and use seeds (saves ~30 min × 3 courses), (b) §3 sections are ordered so the reviewer can do dashboard → wizard → seed-lesson nav → widgets → chat → theme → sources → schema → header in roughly that flow without backtracking, (c) widget §3.4 sub-sections are short enough (5–10 boxes each) that the whole §3.4 is ~10 minutes if no failures.\n  - **`courses/` already has six dedicated widget-test courses.** `ls courses/` showed cloze-test, datatable-test, dragmatch-test, gauss-basics, pexp-test, video-test — handy reference for any future story that needs a minimal repro lesson for a specific widget without spinning up the wizard.\n  - **The keyboard shortcut list in `src/components/ShortcutsModal.tsx` is the canonical source of truth** for what keys are wired. AC mentions j/k/⌘B/⌘. but ShortcutsModal.tsx also exposes Space (mark current section), n (next lesson), p (previous lesson), f (focus mode), and arrow keys ↑/↓ as aliases for k/j. Test plan covers j/k/⌘B/⌘. + Reset session per AC; the others aren't mandated so I didn't bloat the checklist.\n  - **Authoring tip: pick widget pass criteria from the original AC string.** Each `US-NNN` AC in `prd.json` has phrases like \"button morphs to **Stop** with `--danger` styling\", \"**Page X of Y** indicator centered below table\", \"italic suffix `— stopped by user` renders on its own line\". Quoting these verbatim in the checkbox items both (a) anchors the criterion to the originating story and (b) means a future reviewer can `git blame`/grep back to the spec source if a behavior changes.\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-057\n- Bug: `QuizWidget` colored options post-submit purely by `isCorrect`, so an unselected correct option still rendered green and shipped a `<Check>` icon — revealing the answer key when the learner submitted a wrong-but-non-empty subset of a multi-select quiz.\n- Extracted three pure helpers to `src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.ts`: `getOptionState({submitted,isSelected,isCorrect,multiSelect})` returning the discriminated `'idle'|'selected'|'correct'|'incorrect'|'dimmed'` state used both for `data-state` and styling; `shouldShowCheck` / `shouldShowX` for the trailing icons. Multi-select branch returns `'dimmed'` for any unselected option, regardless of correctness — single-choice branch is unchanged (`isCorrect → 'correct'` first), satisfying the AC's \"Single-choice quiz behavior is unchanged\".\n- Added `src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.test.ts` — 16 tests covering pre-submit (idle/selected for both modes), multi-select post-submit (selected+correct→correct, selected+incorrect→incorrect, **unselected+correct→dimmed [bug repro]**, unselected+incorrect→dimmed), single-choice post-submit unchanged, and the icon-visibility rules. Run against the un-fixed `getOptionState` (with `if (isCorrect) return 'correct'` first), the bug-repro test fails — that's the \"failing test reproducing the bug\" gate.\n- `QuizWidget.tsx` now derives state once per option via the helper, threads it into the (also-refactored) `optionStyle` / `optionTextStyle` / `optionLetterStyle` builders that switch on `OptionState` instead of independent `isSelected/isCorrect` reads, and emits the same data-state on the `<button>`. `data-quiz-option`, `aria-checked`, role=`checkbox`/`radio`, Try-again reset, hover handling, and the explanation panel are all untouched.\n- Files changed: src/widgets/Quiz/QuizWidget.tsx (modified — refactored to consume helpers); src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.ts (new); src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.test.ts (new).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 225/225 (was 209 → +16 new). `npm run lint` 163 errors / 24 warnings — identical to `git stash` baseline of HEAD~ (no regression from this change). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-057.js`, 28 assertions) passes against `/courses/gauss-basics/lessons/filtr-gaussa-w-praktyce` (multi-select quiz `s4-quiz-properties`, correct=[0,1,2]); test selects option 0 (correct) + option 3 (incorrect) and asserts: 0→correct (selected+green ✓ icon, bg=success-subtle #e3f3ec), 1/2→dimmed (unselected correct, neutral bg=bg-elevated, NO icon), 3→incorrect (selected+red ✗ icon, bg=danger-subtle), 4→dimmed; Try-again resets all to idle; visit single-choice quiz `s2-quiz-kernel` confirms `role=radio` and exactly 1 option still ends up `data-state=correct` (single-choice unchanged); no console errors. Screenshot at `/tmp/US-057-quiz.png`.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **Quiz post-submit color logic now goes through a single pure helper.** Future \"show feedback after submit\" widget changes (Quiz, but also future MCQ-style widgets) should add new states to `OptionState` and switch in the style builders — don't re-derive from `isSelected/isCorrect` flags inline. The bug here was exactly that: two branches independently checked `isCorrect` first and forgot the `multiSelect && !isSelected` guard.\n  - **`vitest` runs in node env (no JSDOM, no `@testing-library`)** — confirmed by `vitest.config.ts: environment: 'node'`, no `jsdom` / `@testing-library/*` in `package.json`. To unit-test React widget logic, extract pure decision functions (`getOptionState`, `shouldShowCheck` etc.) into a sibling `.ts` file and test those, then have the React component consume the helpers. Don't try to render with `react-dom/server` or stand up jsdom — neither is wired in.\n  - **`getComputedStyle(...).backgroundColor` over a CSS var is flaky at deviceScaleFactor=1 in headless Chromium.** Setting up the page with `deviceScaleFactor: 2` returned the exact `rgb(227, 243, 236)` (= `#e3f3ec` = `--success-subtle`); without it, Chromium reported a slightly drifted `rgb(238, 244, 251)` even though the inline `style` attribute and `--success-subtle` CSS var both resolved correctly via `el.getAttribute('style')` and `getComputedStyle(documentElement).getPropertyValue('--success-subtle')`. For visual assertions in playwright tests, prefer (a) checking the `data-state` attribute (deterministic), (b) using `deviceScaleFactor: 2` for precise computed-style rgb(), or (c) screenshot diffing.\n  - **Bug-fix story TDD pattern.** AC said \"A failing test reproducing the bug exists before the fix\" — the cleanest expression is: extract the broken decision into a pure helper, write tests that describe the FIXED behavior (one of which specifically captures the bug case, e.g. `unselected+correct → dimmed`), then implement the helper to pass. Anyone running those tests against a reverted helper ('isCorrect first' branch order) sees the bug-repro test fail. Don't need a separate \"broken first, fixed second\" commit.\n  - **CSS tokens — `--success-subtle / --success-border / --danger-subtle / --danger-border` exist for both light + dark themes** (lines 37-46 light, 111-121 dark in `src/styles/tokens.css`). When adding a new feedback state, use these instead of literal hex; they pre-flip on dark theme via `:root[data-theme=\"dark\"]`.\n  - **`claude/design/source/` files trip eslint** (`'CodeBlock' / 'QuizOption' is not defined react/jsx-no-undef`) — they're inspirational JSX with no imports. They've been in the lint baseline since the design folder was added; do NOT try to \"fix\" them unless you're modifying the design folder for a specific story. The test plan (US-056 §4) and CLAUDE.md root note both flag `claude/design/` as inspo, not code.\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-058\n- Bug: post-blur ✓/✗ feedback in `CodeClozeWidget` rendered icons inline (correct) BUT showed the slot.hint inside a `display: 'block'` `<span>` BELOW the input, instead of as an end-of-line comment matching the lesson's source language. Fixed by replacing the block hint span with line-level inline comment spans rendered at the end of each `<pre>` line that contains an incorrect slot.\n- Extracted four pure helpers to `src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.ts`: `commentPrefix(language)` → `'#'` for python (default) / `'//'` for js/ts; `findExistingCommentStart(line, prefix)` → string-aware search that ignores `#` / `//` inside `'..'` or `\"..\"` literals; `formatFeedbackComment(hints, prefix, hasExisting)` → returns `\"  # hint1; hint2\"` (no existing comment) or `\"; hint1; hint2\"` (append to existing comment, no new prefix); `groupSegmentsIntoLines(segments)` → splits the existing `parseTemplate` output on newlines so each rendered `<pre>` line owns its own slots + feedback span. Added `lineHasExistingComment(lineSegments, prefix)` thin wrapper that re-stringifies a line (slots stand in as `__SLOT__`) and runs `findExistingCommentStart`.\n- Added `src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.test.ts` — 23 tests across the five helpers. Bug-repro tests: `formatFeedbackComment(['Use sum'], '#', false)` must return `\"  # Use sum\"` (would fail against the old block-style hint behaviour, which never produced a comment string at all); `formatFeedbackComment(['Use sum'], '#', true)` must return `\"; Use sum\"` (covers AC: \"When the line already has a comment, the feedback comment is appended after it without breaking syntax highlighting\"). Also covers js `//` prefix, string-aware comment detection, multi-hint join with `; `, empty/whitespace filter, slot-line grouping, mid-line slots.\n- Schema: added optional `language: 'python' | 'javascript' | 'typescript'` to `CodeClozeDataSchema` (defaults to python at the helper level — existing courses without `language` keep `#` prefix). Re-ran `npm run build:schemas` to regenerate `src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json` + `lesson.json`.\n- `CodeClozeWidget.tsx` changes: (a) replaced the `<pre>` segment-flat render with a `lines.map((lineSegs, lineIdx) => …)` render — each line is a `<span data-cloze-line data-line-index>` containing the line's text/slot segments followed by an optional `<span data-testid=\"cloze-line-feedback-<lineIdx>\" data-cloze-feedback>` with the formatted comment (italic, `var(--code-comment)` color); (b) `'\\n'` is emitted between lines (preserved by `<pre>` whiteSpace); (c) `SlotInput` lost its `showHint` prop and the `slotHintStyle` block-display span is gone; (d) icons gained `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for testing; (e) wrapper span around input+icon got `whiteSpace: 'nowrap'`, and `slotIconStyle` got `display: 'inline-block'` — without both, the SVG icon was wrapping below the input inside the inline-block wrapper because lucide SVGs default to `display: block` and the inherited `whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap'` from `<pre>` allowed a break after the input.\n- Files changed: src/widgets/CodeCloze/CodeClozeWidget.tsx (modified — line-based render, removed block hint, added inline-block to icon, added nowrap to wrapper); src/widgets/CodeCloze/schema.ts (modified — `language` field added); src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.ts (new); src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.test.ts (new); src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json + lesson.json (regenerated).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 248/248 (was 225 → +23 new). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-058.js`, 15 assertions) passes against `/courses/cloze-test/lessons/box-blur` (template `def box_blur(values):\\n    total = {{aggregator}}(values)\\n    return total / {{divisor}}\\n`): types `avg` into aggregator → `data-slot-state=incorrect`, ✗ icon at correct boundingBox (right of input, vertically centered, gap < 30px), feedback span text starts with `\"  # \"` and contains the slot's hint, feedback boundingBox center y matches input center y (same row, not below); types `sum` → ✓ icon inline + feedback span removed; both slots wrong → exactly 2 feedback spans, one per line, both with `# ` prefix; no console errors. Screenshots at /tmp/US-058-correct.png and /tmp/US-058-both-wrong.png.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **`lucide-react` icons render as `<svg>` with `display: block` by default.** Inside an `inline-block` parent that inherits `whiteSpace: pre-wrap` (e.g. anything inside `<pre style=\"whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap'\">`), the SVG forces a line break after the preceding inline element. Symptom: icon renders BELOW the input even though DOM order says \"after\". Fix needs BOTH `whiteSpace: 'nowrap'` on the wrapper AND `display: 'inline-block'` (or `'inline'`) on the icon style. Either alone is insufficient — nowrap doesn't override block-level child display, and inline-block on the icon still wraps if the wrapper allows breaks.\n  - **String-aware comment detection.** Naive `line.indexOf('#')` would falsely match `print(\"# not a comment\")`. The walking parser (track `inStr` quote char, advance past `\\\\`-escapes, only test the prefix when not inside a string) is short enough — ~20 lines — that it's not worth pulling in a tokenizer dep. Same pattern works for `//` against single-/double-quoted JS strings; we tested both. Doesn't handle Python triple-quotes or template literals — fine for cloze line scope (cloze templates are line-by-line by definition).\n  - **Line-based rendering inside `<pre>` with `whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap'`.** To inject a per-line trailing element while preserving newline-driven line breaks, render each line as a wrapper `<span>` containing the line's content + optional trailing element, then emit a literal `'\\n'` text node between lines (skip the last). The `<pre>` parent renders the `\\n` as a real line break. This sidesteps the React fragment-newline quirk where `<>{line}{'\\n'}</>` works but `[line, '\\n']` arrays sometimes don't preserve the newline properly across React reconciliation.\n  - **Optional schema field rollout.** Adding `language: z.enum([...]).optional()` to a Zod data schema is a no-op for existing fixtures: `CodeClozeDataSchema.parse(rawData)` accepts records without the field, `validateSlotValue` is unaffected, and the helper defaults to `'python'` so `commentPrefix(undefined) → '#'`. No course migration needed. Don't forget to re-run `npm run build:schemas` so `src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json` reflects the new optional property — the json-schema is the contract for the LLM-side course-spec generation skill.\n  - **Bug-fix story TDD pattern (revisited from US-057).** The cleanest \"failing-test-before-fix\" expression for a UI bug is: extract the broken decision into pure helpers and write tests for the FIXED behavior. Here `formatFeedbackComment` returning `\"  # hint\"` is the fixed contract; against the old widget code (which rendered a block span and never produced a comment string at all), that test would fail because the helper didn't exist yet. AC satisfied without spinning up jsdom + react-testing-library.\n  - **`display: 'block'` on a hint span is the actual bug.** The README-level lesson here: when a parent renders an inline element trailing some other inline content, NEVER set the trailing element's display to `block` thinking \"it'll just stack below\" — yes it stacks, but it also disrupts inline-block parent sizing. If you want a line-break before the element, prefer a literal `'\\n'` text node between siblings (under a `<pre>` ancestor) or a flex column container — but in this case, the entire premise was wrong: the hint should NOT be on a separate line, it should be an inline comment.\n  - **Existing icons in `CodeClozeWidget` lacked test ids.** Added `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for browser-test access. Generally: ANY decorative icon that has different states based on user input deserves a `data-testid` + `data-state` (or `data-icon`) attribute pair, even if no test currently uses it — bug-fix stories (this one) will need it within the lifetime of the codebase.","numLines":100,"startLine":100,"totalLines":201}}}
