{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BBERNiXThxkCCei15c9WWR","type":"tool_result","content":"196\t  - **Optional schema field rollout.** Adding `language: z.enum([...]).optional()` to a Zod data schema is a no-op for existing fixtures: `CodeClozeDataSchema.parse(rawData)` accepts records without the field, `validateSlotValue` is unaffected, and the helper defaults to `'python'` so `commentPrefix(undefined) → '#'`. No course migration needed. Don't forget to re-run `npm run build:schemas` so `src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json` reflects the new optional property — the json-schema is the contract for the LLM-side course-spec generation skill.\n197\t  - **Bug-fix story TDD pattern (revisited from US-057).** The cleanest \"failing-test-before-fix\" expression for a UI bug is: extract the broken decision into pure helpers and write tests for the FIXED behavior. Here `formatFeedbackComment` returning `\"  # hint\"` is the fixed contract; against the old widget code (which rendered a block span and never produced a comment string at all), that test would fail because the helper didn't exist yet. AC satisfied without spinning up jsdom + react-testing-library.\n198\t  - **`display: 'block'` on a hint span is the actual bug.** The README-level lesson here: when a parent renders an inline element trailing some other inline content, NEVER set the trailing element's display to `block` thinking \"it'll just stack below\" — yes it stacks, but it also disrupts inline-block parent sizing. If you want a line-break before the element, prefer a literal `'\\n'` text node between siblings (under a `<pre>` ancestor) or a flex column container — but in this case, the entire premise was wrong: the hint should NOT be on a separate line, it should be an inline comment.\n199\t  - **Existing icons in `CodeClozeWidget` lacked test ids.** Added `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for browser-test access. Generally: ANY decorative icon that has different states based on user input deserves a `data-testid` + `data-state` (or `data-icon`) attribute pair, even if no test currently uses it — bug-fix stories (this one) will need it within the lifetime of the codebase.\n200\t---"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"346a3f37-7cc3-4519-9057-276571b4b70f","uuid":"9b21f0a8-288c-464c-9fee-8ef36369d5b0","timestamp":"2026-05-04T08:19:35.973Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/nukeemann/projects/ai_lecturer/.worktrees/US-059/scripts/ralph/progress.txt","content":"  - **Optional schema field rollout.** Adding `language: z.enum([...]).optional()` to a Zod data schema is a no-op for existing fixtures: `CodeClozeDataSchema.parse(rawData)` accepts records without the field, `validateSlotValue` is unaffected, and the helper defaults to `'python'` so `commentPrefix(undefined) → '#'`. No course migration needed. Don't forget to re-run `npm run build:schemas` so `src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json` reflects the new optional property — the json-schema is the contract for the LLM-side course-spec generation skill.\n  - **Bug-fix story TDD pattern (revisited from US-057).** The cleanest \"failing-test-before-fix\" expression for a UI bug is: extract the broken decision into pure helpers and write tests for the FIXED behavior. Here `formatFeedbackComment` returning `\"  # hint\"` is the fixed contract; against the old widget code (which rendered a block span and never produced a comment string at all), that test would fail because the helper didn't exist yet. AC satisfied without spinning up jsdom + react-testing-library.\n  - **`display: 'block'` on a hint span is the actual bug.** The README-level lesson here: when a parent renders an inline element trailing some other inline content, NEVER set the trailing element's display to `block` thinking \"it'll just stack below\" — yes it stacks, but it also disrupts inline-block parent sizing. If you want a line-break before the element, prefer a literal `'\\n'` text node between siblings (under a `<pre>` ancestor) or a flex column container — but in this case, the entire premise was wrong: the hint should NOT be on a separate line, it should be an inline comment.\n  - **Existing icons in `CodeClozeWidget` lacked test ids.** Added `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for browser-test access. Generally: ANY decorative icon that has different states based on user input deserves a `data-testid` + `data-state` (or `data-icon`) attribute pair, even if no test currently uses it — bug-fix stories (this one) will need it within the lifetime of the codebase.\n---","numLines":5,"startLine":196,"totalLines":201}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01RQgYaobywj8XysVjq1UiL2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VuQgNXFd6netDtDwSuWjv4","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/home/nukeemann/projects/ai_lecturer/.worktrees/US-059/scripts/ralph/progress.txt","old_string":"  - **Existing icons in `CodeClozeWidget` lacked test ids.** Added `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for browser-test access. Generally: ANY decorative icon that has different states based on user input deserves a `data-testid` + `data-state` (or `data-icon`) attribute pair, even if no test currently uses it — bug-fix stories (this one) will need it within the lifetime of the codebase.\n---","new_string":"  - **Existing icons in `CodeClozeWidget` lacked test ids.** Added `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for browser-test access. Generally: ANY decorative icon that has different states based on user input deserves a `data-testid` + `data-state` (or `data-icon`) attribute pair, even if no test currently uses it — bug-fix stories (this one) will need it within the lifetime of the codebase.\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-059\n- Bug: `ShortcutsModal` and the toolbar tutor button + chat composer hint hard-coded macOS glyphs (`⌘`, `⌥`, `⌃`, `⇧`) regardless of platform — Windows/Linux learners saw symbols their keyboards don't have. The keybind handler side was actually already correct: `isMod` in `src/lib/hooks/useKeyboardShortcuts.ts` accepts BOTH `metaKey` and `ctrlKey`, so `Mod-B` / `Mod-.` already fire for both Cmd on macOS and Ctrl on Windows/Linux. Bug was purely on the display side, plus there was zero test coverage proving `isMod` accepts ctrlKey.\n- Added `src/lib/platform/platform.ts` — pure helpers: `isMacPlatform(userAgent?)` (regex `/Mac|iPhone|iPad|iPod/i` against `navigator.userAgent`, defaults to non-mac when ua unavailable so SSR is safe), `KeyToken` type (`'mod'|'alt'|'shift'|'ctrl'|'enter'|'tab'|'space'|'esc'|'up'|'down'|'left'|'right'` plus literals), `keyLabel(token, isMac)` returns the platform-correct label (mac: `⌘/⌥/⇧/⌃/↵`; non-mac: `Ctrl/Alt/Shift/Ctrl/↵` — `↵` kept on both since it's a universal Enter glyph), `modLabel(isMac)` for inline hints (`⌘` vs `Ctrl`).\n- Added `src/lib/platform/useIsMacPlatform.ts` — SSR-safe React hook. Returns `false` on first render (matches SSR + initial hydration) then flips after `useEffect` reads `navigator.userAgent`. The non-mac default is correct for the majority of users; macOS users see one frame of \"Ctrl\" before swap. Uses an `eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/set-state-in-effect` directive for the canonical one-shot detection pattern.\n- Added `src/lib/platform/platform.test.ts` — 18 unit tests. Bug-repro group \"keyLabel — Windows / Linux (bug repro for US-059)\" asserts `keyLabel('mod', false) === 'Ctrl'` AND `!== '⌘'` for each of mod/alt/shift/ctrl. Run against a hypothetical \"always return mac glyph\" implementation, all four assertions fail — that's the failing-test-before-fix gate. Plus macOS ua detection (Macintosh + iPhone/iPad/iPod), Windows + Linux + Android negative cases, empty ua → false, all the literal pass-through cases.\n- Added `src/lib/hooks/useKeyboardShortcuts.test.ts` — 4 tests for `isMod`. Bug-repro: \"returns true when ctrlKey is held (Windows / Linux Ctrl)\" — a pre-fix Cmd-only implementation would fail. Plus metaKey, neither, both.\n- `src/components/ShortcutsModal.tsx` — `ShortcutSpec.keys` typed as `KeyToken[]` (was `string[]`); GROUPS uses semantic tokens (`'mod'`, `'shift'`, `'esc'`, `'space'`, `'enter'`, `'up'`, `'down'`, `'tab'`) instead of literal glyphs. Modal calls `useIsMacPlatform()` and threads `isMac` through `Group` → `Row` → `keyLabel` so each `<Kbd>` gets the platform-appropriate label. Modal root gains `data-platform=\"mac\"|\"non-mac\"` for browser-test assertion. Existing `data-testid=\"kbd\"` is preserved.\n- `src/components/LessonChat.tsx` — chat composer hint changed from hard-coded `'⌘+Enter to send'` to `<span data-testid=\"lesson-chat-send-hint\">{modLabel(isMac)}+Enter to send</span>`. The `isMod` predicate inside `handleKeyDown` (`e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey`) was already correct cross-platform; not changed.\n- `src/app/courses/[slug]/lessons/[lessonSlug]/page.tsx` — extracted the inline `⌘.` toolbar glyph into a new `TutorToolbarButton` subcomponent that calls `useIsMacPlatform()` and renders `⌘.` on mac vs `Ctrl+.` on Windows/Linux (added `+` separator on non-mac to match Windows/Linux convention; on mac the bare `⌘.` keeps Apple's no-separator convention). Glyph wrapper gets `data-testid=\"tutor-btn-shortcut\"`. The keyboard-shortcut handlers in this page (`Mod-B`, `Mod-.`, j/k/↑/↓, etc.) all use `isMod(e)` which already accepts ctrlKey — no logic change needed.\n- `src/app/test-code-runner/page.tsx` — also updated the dev-test-page title from `\"Try it: print('hi'), then ⌘+Enter\"` to template-literal `${modLabel(isMac)}+Enter` for consistency. Page is dev-only.\n- Files changed: src/lib/platform/platform.ts (new); src/lib/platform/platform.test.ts (new — 18 tests); src/lib/platform/useIsMacPlatform.ts (new); src/lib/hooks/useKeyboardShortcuts.test.ts (new — 4 tests); src/components/ShortcutsModal.tsx (modified — semantic tokens + platform-aware rendering); src/components/LessonChat.tsx (modified — modLabel(isMac) hint + testid); src/app/courses/[slug]/lessons/[lessonSlug]/page.tsx (modified — TutorToolbarButton + imports); src/app/test-code-runner/page.tsx (modified — modLabel hint).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 272/272 (was 248 → +24 new: 18 platform.test + 4 useKeyboardShortcuts.test + 2 fixed by toBe(false) sanity). `npm run lint` 162 errors / 24 warnings — IDENTICAL to baseline 162/24 from `git stash` of HEAD~ (no regression). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-059.js`, 24 assertions across two contexts) passes against `/courses/gauss-basics/lessons/wprowadzenie-do-filtrowania`. Windows context (UA `Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64...`): modal `data-platform=\"non-mac\"`, modal text contains NO ⌘/⌥/⌃/⇧ glyphs, `<kbd>` labels include `Ctrl` + `Shift` (not `⌘` / `⇧`), tutor button shows `Ctrl+.`, chat hint is `Ctrl+Enter to send`, `Ctrl+B` toggles TOC (`data-collapsed false → true`), no console errors. macOS context (UA `Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X...`): modal `data-platform=\"mac\"`, modal contains both ⌘ and ⇧ glyphs, modal text does NOT contain \" Ctrl \", `<kbd>` labels include `⌘` + `⇧`, tutor button shows `⌘.`, chat hint is `⌘+Enter to send`, `Cmd+B` (Meta+B) toggles TOC, no console errors. Screenshots at /tmp/US-059-win.png and /tmp/US-059-mac.png.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **`isMod` was already platform-flexible** — `e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey` is the canonical \"Mod\" predicate. Anyone reading the AC (\"the keybinding handlers only listen for Cmd\") might assume the handler logic is broken and try to fix it; in fact only the display layer was wrong. The `useKeyboardShortcuts.test.ts` file added now locks this in, so a future change that \"simplifies\" `isMod` to just `e.metaKey` will break the suite.\n  - **Platform detection from `navigator.userAgent` is acceptable for shortcuts.** UA spoofing exists, but the worst case is that someone with a spoofed UA sees the \"wrong\" glyph — they still get a working modal because `isMod` accepts both modifiers. Don't reach for `navigator.platform` (deprecated), `navigator.userAgentData.platform` (Chromium-only), or feature detection — UA-string regex is the simplest correct answer here.\n  - **SSR-safe hook pattern: `useState(false) + useEffect(() => set(...))`.** Avoid `useState(() => typeof window !== 'undefined' && ...)` because Next 16's hydration phase runs the lazy initializer with `window` defined on the client but the SSR HTML was rendered with `false` — that's a hydration mismatch. Always default to the SSR value (`false` here) and flip in `useEffect`. The 1-frame flash on Mac is the price of zero hydration warnings.\n  - **Semantic tokens > literal glyphs in shortcut descriptors.** Rather than `keys: ['⌘', 'B']`, use `keys: ['mod', 'B']` and translate at render time. Future changes (a new platform, a new keymap variant for vim users, etc.) only touch `keyLabel()`, not every shortcut definition. `KeyToken` is a string literal union with a `(string & {})` fallback so literal keys like `'B'`, `'.'`, `'?'` pass through verbatim — no breaking change to call sites that mix tokens and literals.\n  - **Glyph asymmetry: `⌘.` vs `Ctrl+.`** — Mac convention is no separator (Apple menus), Windows/Linux convention is `+`. The TutorToolbarButton now matches both. Don't try to unify to `⌘+.` \"for consistency\" — it'll look wrong on Mac. The textContent test asserts the EXACT string per platform.\n  - **`↵` Enter glyph stays cross-platform.** AC only mentions ⌘/⌥/⌃ (and by inclusion ⇧) — `↵` is a universal Return symbol on both Mac and Windows keyboards. Replacing it with the word \"Enter\" would expand the `<kbd>` width unnecessarily and break vertical alignment in the modal rows. Same for arrow glyphs (`↑↓←→`), Tab, Space, Esc — these read fine on both platforms.\n  - **`page.evaluate(() => navigator.userAgent)` returns the UA the test assigned**, not the host's UA. So passing `userAgent: WINDOWS_UA` to `browser.newContext({...})` is enough to make `useIsMacPlatform()` return `false` inside the test page — no need to mock `navigator` directly. Verified: macOS context returned `data-platform=\"mac\"`, Windows context `data-platform=\"non-mac\"` from the same dev server.\n  - **TOC selector is `data-testid=\"lesson-toc\"` not `\"toc\"`.** Mistook the convention initially — every TOC-related testid is prefixed `lesson-toc-…` or `toc-<part>`. The full list is in page.tsx:1343 (root), 1399 (header), 1448 (toggle), 1496 (modules list), etc. Use `lesson-toc[data-collapsed]` for assertions about the panel's open/collapsed state.\n---"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":926,"cache_read_input_tokens":131648,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":926},"output_tokens":72,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"346a3f37-7cc3-4519-9057-276571b4b70f","uuid":"61ec8d69-675f-4226-9d82-21937cde3a3d"}
