 16's hydration phase runs the lazy initializer with `window` defined on the client but the SSR HTML was rendered with `false` — that's a hydration mismatch. Always default to the SSR value (`false` here) and flip in `useEffect`. The 1-frame flash on Mac is the price of zero hydration warnings.\n  - **Semantic tokens > literal glyphs in shortcut descriptors.** Rather than `keys: ['⌘', 'B']`, use `keys: ['mod', 'B']` and translate at render time. Future changes (a new platform, a new keymap variant for vim users, etc.) only touch `keyLabel()`, not every shortcut definition. `KeyToken` is a string literal union with a `(string & {})` fallback so literal keys like `'B'`, `'.'`, `'?'` pass through verbatim — no breaking change to call sites that mix tokens and literals.\n  - **Glyph asymmetry: `⌘.` vs `Ctrl+.`** — Mac convention is no separator (Apple menus), Windows/Linux convention is `+`. The TutorToolbarButton now matches both. Don't try to unify to `⌘+.` \"for consistency\" — it'll look wrong on Mac. The textContent test asserts the EXACT string per platform.\n  - **`↵` Enter glyph stays cross-platform.** AC only mentions ⌘/⌥/⌃ (and by inclusion ⇧) — `↵` is a universal Return symbol on both Mac and Windows keyboards. Replacing it with the word \"Enter\" would expand the `<kbd>` width unnecessarily and break vertical alignment in the modal rows. Same for arrow glyphs (`↑↓←→`), Tab, Space, Esc — these read fine on both platforms.\n  - **`page.evaluate(() => navigator.userAgent)` returns the UA the test assigned**, not the host's UA. So passing `userAgent: WINDOWS_UA` to `browser.newContext({...})` is enough to make `useIsMacPlatform()` return `false` inside the test page — no need to mock `navigator` directly. Verified: macOS context returned `data-platform=\"mac\"`, Windows context `data-platform=\"non-mac\"` from the same dev server.\n  - **TOC selector is `data-testid=\"lesson-toc\"` not `\"toc\"`.** Mistook the convention initially — every TOC-related testid is prefixed `lesson-toc-…` or `toc-<part>`. The full list is in page.tsx:1343 (root), 1399 (header), 1448 (toggle), 1496 (modules list), etc. Use `lesson-toc[data-collapsed]` for assertions about the panel's open/collapsed state.\n---","originalFile":"# Ralph Progress Log\nRotated: Mon May  4 00:43:02 CEST 2026\nPrevious log archived to: archive/progress-2026-05-04-0043.txt\n---\n\n## Codebase Patterns\n- Repo dir is named `US-001` (capitalized) → cannot run `create-next-app .` directly because npm rejects capital letters in package names. Workaround: scaffold into a temp dir (e.g. `/tmp/ai-lecturer-scaffold`), then copy files into the worktree (skip the inner `.git`), and set the package name in `package.json` manually.\n- `shadcn@latest` (v4.x) emits Tailwind v4 / `base-nova` style by default — incompatible with a Tailwind v3 project. Use `shadcn@2.3.0` for v3-compatible init, or write `components.json` by hand.\n- Tailwind v3 baseline = `@tailwind base; @tailwind components; @tailwind utilities;` in `globals.css` + `tailwind.config.ts` with `content: [\"./src/**/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx,mdx}\"]` + `postcss.config.js` with `tailwindcss` and `autoprefixer` plugins. Theme/token wiring belongs in US-002, not US-001.\n- Design tokens live in `src/styles/tokens.css` (canonical source from `claude/design/source/tokens.css`) and are imported at the top of `src/app/globals.css`. Tailwind utilities resolve through CSS vars, so changing a `--*` value at runtime updates Tailwind classes too.\n- Theme/accent/density are switched by `data-theme` / `data-accent` / `data-density` attributes on `<html>` (defaults: `light` / `default` / `comfortable`). Selectors in tokens.css are scoped to `:root[data-theme=…]` etc. — adding new variants means adding a new `:root[data-…=\"…\"]` block, not a new file.\n- Geist Sans + Mono are loaded via `next/font` (`geist/font/sans`, `geist/font/mono`) in `src/app/layout.tsx`. Their `.variable` classNames define `--font-geist-sans` / `--font-geist-mono`; tokens.css references those via `var(--font-geist-sans)` inside `--font-sans` / `--font-prose` / `--font-display`. Don't hardcode font names anywhere else.\n- Playwright skill at `scripts/ralph/skills/playwright-skill/` ships without `node_modules`; run `npm install --no-audit --no-fund` inside it before first use. Its `detectDevServers()` helper missed our :3000 dev server even when up — fall back to setting `TARGET_URL` manually in the test script if detection fails.\n- **Zod is pinned to v3** (`^3.25.x`) because `zod-to-json-schema` returns an empty object on Zod v4. Don't bump zod to v4 unless the `zod-to-json-schema` import also changes. (Zod v4 has a native `z.toJSONSchema()`, but our AC mandates the third-party lib.)\n- **Vitest needs its own alias config.** `vitest.config.ts` must declare `resolve.alias = { '@': fileURLToPath(new URL('./src', import.meta.url)) }`. Vitest does NOT read `tsconfig.json#paths`. Next + `tsc` + `tsx` (used by `npm run build:schemas`) DO auto-pick it up.\n- **Eslint config does not exempt `_`-prefixed unused vars.** For \"drop one field\" test fixtures, use `const broken: Partial<typeof obj> = { ...obj }; delete broken.field;` — destructuring with `_name` triggers `@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars` warnings.\n- **Schema layout.** Source-of-truth Zod schemas live in two places: per-widget data shapes at `src/widgets/<Name>/schema.ts`; everything else (Course, Module, Lesson, Section discriminated union, Progress, CourseSpec) at `src/lib/schemas/`. `SectionSchema` is a `z.discriminatedUnion('type', […])` over `'theory'|'quiz'|'code'|'demo'|'sandbox'|'custom'`. Adding a new widget = new folder under `src/widgets/<Name>/` + register a variant in `src/lib/schemas/lesson.ts` + add it to the `widgets` map in `src/widgets/schemas/build.ts`. `npm run build:schemas` regenerates the committed `*.json` files in `src/widgets/schemas/`.\n- **Filesystem helpers** live in `src/lib/server/` (server-only — never import from a `'use client'` module). `paths.ts` exports `coursesRoot()`, `assertSafeSlug()`, `slugify()`, `courseDir/courseFile/courseSpecFile/lessonFile()`. `atomic.ts` exports `atomicWriteJson(path, data)` (writes to `<path>.tmp` then `fs.rename`). `coursesRoot()` honours `COURSES_ROOT_OVERRIDE` env var (used in tests); production reads `path.join(process.cwd(), 'courses')`. Disk layout: `courses/<slug>/{course.json, course-spec.json, lessons/<lessonSlug>.json}`.\n- **Next 16 route handlers + vitest.** Tests can import handlers directly from `src/app/api/.../route.ts` and call them with a synthesised `Request` and `{ params: Promise.resolve({...}) }` (Next 15+ wraps params in a Promise). `NextResponse.json()` returns a real `Response`, so `await res.json()` and `res.status` work in tests. Put the test file OUTSIDE `src/app/` (e.g. `src/lib/server/api.test.ts`) so Next doesn't try to compile it as a route — vitest's `include: 'src/**/*.test.ts'` still picks it up.\n- **Slug rules.** `assertSafeSlug` rejects empty strings and any slug containing `/`, `\\`, or `..` (path-traversal protection). Public slugs are derived from user input via `slugify()` (NFKD-normalise → strip combining marks → lowercase → `[_\\s]+→-` → strip non-`[a-z0-9-]` → collapse `-+` → trim). Slugify can return `''` for all-punctuation inputs; callers must guard (POST `/api/courses` returns 400 in that case).\n- **Pyodide worker singleton.** Web Workers are bundled by Turbopack via `new Worker(new URL('./worker.ts', import.meta.url))` (NO `type: 'module'` → classic worker, can use `importScripts` for CDN bootstrap). The worker file at `src/lib/pyodide/worker.ts` is excluded from main thread imports by virtue of being instantiated only via the URL constructor. Client hook lives at `src/lib/pyodide/client.ts` (`'use client'`) and exposes `usePyodide()`; the worker is a module-scope singleton created lazily on first hook subscription via `useSyncExternalStore`. Subscription on mount = lazy-load contract: dashboards / SSR never trigger worker creation. For id-keyed promise resolution: each `run`/`runWithTests` call generates `req-N`, stores `{resolve, reject}` in a `Map`, and pops on matching `event.data.id`. Custom test runner (no pytest) lives in a Python `__ai_run_tests` function injected once per worker; it `exec()`s each test body in a fresh copy of the user namespace and catches `AssertionError` separately for `assertionDetail`. Test results array shape: `[{name, passed, traceback?, assertionDetail?}]`.\n- **CodeMirror 6 setup.** Packages: `codemirror @codemirror/state @codemirror/view @codemirror/lang-python @codemirror/commands @codemirror/language @codemirror/autocomplete` (commands brings `defaultKeymap`/`history`/`historyKeymap`/`indentWithTab`; language brings `HighlightStyle`/`syntaxHighlighting`/`bracketMatching`/`indentOnInput`). Theme via `EditorView.theme({...})` consuming our `--code-bg/--code-text/--code-keyword/...` CSS vars. Highlight via `HighlightStyle.define([{ tag: t.keyword, color: 'var(--code-keyword)' }, ...])` from `@lezer/highlight` tags. Editor instantiated once in `useEffect(() => {...}, [])` with `EditorState.create({doc, extensions:[…]})`; subsequent doc updates flow via `view.dispatch({changes:{from,to,insert}})`, NOT by re-rendering. Use `EditorView.updateListener.of((u)=>u.docChanged && setCode(u.state.doc.toString()))` to mirror doc into React state.\n- **eslint react-hooks/refs rule is strict.** Never write `someRef.current = value` at component top level — must be inside `useEffect(() => { someRef.current = value; }, [value])` or inside an event handler. Pattern for \"always-fresh closure\" inside a handler set up once: declare `const valueRef = useRef(value); useEffect(() => { valueRef.current = value; }, [value]);` then read `valueRef.current` inside the long-lived closure.\n- **Cmd/Ctrl+Enter in CodeMirror.** Use `keymap.of([{ key: 'Mod-Enter', preventDefault: true, run: () => { ...; return true; } }])` and put it BEFORE the `defaultKeymap` keymap so it wins over Cm-Enter defaults. `Mod` resolves to ⌘ on Mac and Ctrl elsewhere. Have the `run` callback dispatch via a ref (`handleRunRef.current()`) so it always picks up the latest closure.\n- **Progress PATCH for sectionState.** PATCH `/api/progress` with body `{ courseSlug, lessonSlug, sectionState: { [sectionId]: { userCode } } }`. To CLEAR a section's state (e.g. on Reset), send `{ [sectionId]: {} }` — the server's `applyProgressPatch` shallow-merges into existing `sectionState[sectionId]` then replaces it, so `{}` overwrites and effectively wipes the entry's userCode. Caveat: this also wipes any sibling fields (quizSubmission) on the same section, which is fine because section IDs are per-widget-instance. To mark a section \"done\", PATCH `{ userCode, done: true }` together — `done?: boolean` is the per-section completion flag (added in US-011); sending `{ done: true }` alone would wipe userCode.\n- **CodeRunner slots (US-011).** CodeRunner exposes slot props for graded variants without re-implementing the editor/footer: `extraPanel?: ReactNode` (rendered between output panel and footer), `primaryAction?: { label, icon, onClick, disabled, variant: 'primary'|'secondary'|'ghost', testId, ariaLabel }` (replaces ▶ Run; pass `null` to hide it entirely), `onCodeChange?: (code) => void` (mirror doc into parent state), `onReset?: () => void` (parent can also reset its own state when CodeRunner's Reset fires), `outputPlaceholder?: string`. CodeRunner also exports `codeRunnerEditorTheme` and `codeRunnerHighlightStyle` so child components (e.g. inline read-only test bodies) match the editor's look. Read-only CodeMirror needs BOTH `EditorState.readOnly.of(true)` and `EditorView.editable.of(false)`.\n- **TS target ES2017.** The regex `s` (dotAll) flag triggers `TS1501`. Use `[\\s\\S]` in patterns that need to span newlines, never `/.../s`.\n- **Lesson navigation.** Flat ordered lesson list = `course.modules.flatMap(m => m.lessons)`. Prev/next of current lesson is index-1 / index+1 in that list, crossing module boundaries. Client navigation uses `router.push(\\`/courses/\\${slug}/lessons/\\${targetSlug}\\`)` from `next/navigation`'s `useRouter()`. Mark-finished is `PATCH /api/progress { courseSlug, lessonSlug, status: 'finished' }` — applyProgressPatch already handles this and stamps `finishedAt`.\n- **Adding a new widget type = 5 mandatory wiring points** (see `src/widgets/README.md`): (1) `--widget-<name>` CSS var in both light + dark blocks of `src/styles/tokens.css`; (2) Zod data schema at `src/widgets/<Name>/schema.ts` + register in `src/widgets/schemas/build.ts` so `npm run build:schemas` emits `<name>.json`; (3) `widgetRegistry` entry in `src/widgets/registry.ts` (component / label / icon / accentVar) + extend the `WidgetType` union; (4) `<Name>Editor.tsx` per US-023 pattern + import + branch in both `WidgetEditPanel` and `SectionRenderer` in `src/app/courses/[slug]/lessons/[lessonSlug]/page.tsx`; (5) `<Name>SectionSchema` variant in `SectionSchema` discriminated union at `src/lib/schemas/lesson.ts`. Skipping (5) means widget renders but lesson JSON containing it fails API validation. Histogram (US-030) is the canonical reference implementation.\n- **React-aware playwright slider input.** Setting `el.value` directly bypasses React's value tracker — the `onChange` handler sees the *previous* value. Use the native setter override: `Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(window.HTMLInputElement.prototype, 'value').set.call(el, '4'); el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));`. Same pattern works for `<select>`, `<textarea>`.\n- **Pyodide lesson namespace + test isolation (US-031).** Worker holds a Python-side module-level dict `__ai_lesson_globals` (sourced from `src/lib/pyodide/runnerPython.ts` so vitest can re-install it via the `pyodide` npm dev-dep). All `'run'` calls exec'd with `runPythonAsync(code, { globals: lessonGlobalsProxy })` so the namespace persists across Code/Sandbox widgets within a lesson; `'runWithTests'` exec's user code in lessonGlobals (so helpers persist) but each test body in `dict(__ai_lesson_globals)` (so test mutations don't leak). `'resetNamespace'` (new message type, payload `{ lessonSlug }`) calls `__ai_reset_namespace()` which `.clear()`s in place — DON'T reassign the dict, the JS-side proxy points at the original object. Lesson page calls `usePyodide().resetNamespace(lessonSlug)` from a `useEffect([lessonSlug])` so the namespace wipes on every lesson navigation; toolbar `[data-testid=\"reset-session-btn\"]` exposes a manual reset with a `[data-testid=\"session-toast\"]` ack.\n- **Browser tests touching Code/Sandbox widgets must clear `~/.ai-lecturer/progress.json` per-section state first.** Persisted `userCode` (from US-011) auto-loads as `initialCode`, so a stale entry from a prior aborted run silently shapes Submit outcomes. Wipe via `node -e` JSON edit or use a throwaway course slug.\n- **CodeMirror typing in playwright auto-indents on `\\n`.** `keyboard.type('def f():\\\\n    return 42\\\\n')` produces compound indents. Use single-line lambdas where possible; otherwise insert `Enter` + `Home` between lines, or paste via `view.dispatch({changes:{...}})`.\n- **Pyodide batched stdout strips `\\n` (US-032).** `setStdout({ batched })` fires once per line, content only — never join chunks with `''` or consecutive `print()`s collapse into one line. Use the `formatStdoutChunks` helper at `src/lib/pyodide/stdout.ts`: joins with `\\n`, then per-line keeps only the substring after the last `\\r` so terminal-style in-place updates work and bare `\\r`s don't render as extra line breaks under `pre-wrap`. The CodeRunner output panel already uses `whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap'`, so once newlines actually exist in the text, multi-line output renders correctly.\n- **Worker plot capture (US-045 `runWithPlotParam`).** Matplotlib loaded lazily on first request via `loadPackage(['matplotlib'])` then `matplotlib.use('AGG')` (no display server in worker). Cache namespaces by `setupCode` STRING as the key (`__ai_pexp_namespaces[setup_code]`) so identical setupCode runs once per session — the cached dict is copied with `dict(cached)` for each render so user mutations don't leak. Capture the most-recent figure with `plt.figure(plt.get_fignums()[-1]).savefig(buf, format='png', bbox_inches='tight', dpi=110)` then `plt.close('all')` before the next render. JS plain-object payload (`{name: number|string|boolean}`) crosses the JS→Py boundary as a JsProxy → `params.to_py()` yields a Python dict; types are preserved automatically (number→float, bool→bool, string→str).\n- **Next dev (Turbopack) can hang under back-to-back playwright runs.** Symptom: first navigation succeeds, second test run's `page.goto` times out and `curl` to ANY route hangs indefinitely. Recovery: `pkill -f \"next dev\"` and restart on a different port. Suspect dangling chromium HMR socket from the previous run.\n- **react-hooks/set-state-in-effect rule.** A bare `void asyncFn()` inside `useEffect` triggers this even if setState happens after `await` (the linter doesn't follow async boundaries). Gate the call behind a one-shot `useRef(false)` flag (`if (initialRenderRef.current) return; initialRenderRef.current = true; void asyncFn();`) — the linter sees this as a one-time subscription and accepts it. Same gating pattern that GaussDemo uses with its `pendingSigmaRef` ref-check.\n- **Mark a non-Code section \"done\".** PATCH `/api/progress` with `{ courseSlug, lessonSlug, sectionState: { [sectionId]: { done: true } } }`. `applyProgressPatch` shallow-merges into `sectionState[sectionId]`, so sibling fields on other sections are untouched. Section IDs are per-widget-instance, so this is the canonical pattern when there's no `userCode` to persist (Quiz, CodeCloze, DragMatch, etc.).\n- **Mixing CSS shorthand `border` with separate `borderColor` re-renders trigger a React style warning.** Don't compose `{ border: '1px solid X' }` with a hover override `{ borderColor: 'Y' }`. Use longhand from the start (`borderWidth`, `borderStyle`, `borderColor`) so hover/feedback states only override the longhand piece. Surfaces this in DragMatch zone/bank styles where hover swaps the colour.\n- **dnd-kit (`@dnd-kit/core` + `@dnd-kit/sortable`) is already in `package.json` (added pre-MVP).** `useSensors(useSensor(PointerSensor, { activationConstraint: { distance: 4 } }), useSensor(KeyboardSensor))` is the canonical sensor set for keyboard-accessible drag widgets. `useDraggable` automatically attaches `role=\"button\"`, `tabIndex=0`, `aria-pressed`, `aria-roledescription` — verifying those attributes is enough to prove keyboard sensors are wired without simulating arrow-key navigation in playwright (each ArrowKey only moves 25 px by default and the simulated drag is brittle). For a draggable+droppable bank/zone layout: track `placement: Record<containerId, itemId[]>` with a sentinel `__bank__` id; on `onDragEnd` find the item's source container, splice it out, push into the target. Use `<DragOverlay>` so the drag preview is portal-rendered and unaffected by transforms inside the original layout.\n- **Inline syntax-highlight for cloze-style widgets.** Don't pull in CodeMirror with `Decoration.replace` widgets when the only requirement is \"syntax-highlighted Python with inline editable inputs\" — a small regex-based tokenizer (keywords / strings / comments / numbers / fn-call) inside `<pre>` is enough, and React-managed `<input>`s blur naturally without bridging CM state. CodeCloze's `tokenizePython` is the reference implementation. Use CodeMirror only when the user types into the code itself (Code, Sandbox, ParametricExplorer's Python editors).\n- **Status badge selector for browser tests.** `[data-section-id=\"<id>\"] [data-status=\"done\"]` (NOT `data-widget-status`). `StatusBadge` in `src/widgets/Widget.tsx` exposes the value via `data-status`.\n- **`type ComponentType` import for `widgetRegistry[type].component`.** When the lesson page's `SectionRenderer` forwards optional widget-specific props (e.g. `progressKey`, `onComplete`) via the registry, narrow the registry's `ComponentType<{ data?: unknown }>` with a local cast: `widgetRegistry.codeCloze.component as ComponentType<{ data?: unknown; progressKey?: typeof progressKey; onComplete?: () => void }>`. Add `type ComponentType` to the existing `from 'react'` import block in `page.tsx`.\n- **Avoid `useEffect`+`setState` for \"clamp paging index when filter shrinks rows\".** The `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect` rule rejects `useEffect(() => setPage(Math.max(0, totalPages-1)))`. Instead, derive a `clampedPage = Math.min(page, totalPages-1)` value during render and use it for slicing/indicator/disabled-state — let `setPage` only flip on actual user click. This keeps the linter happy and removes a redundant render cycle when filters narrow the result set.\n- **Sticky thead inside an `overflow:auto` scroll container.** `position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 1` on `<thead>` plus a non-transparent `background` on each `<th>` (or on the `<thead>` itself) is enough — no JS needed. Wrap the `<table>` in a `div` with `maxHeight: '60vh'; overflow: auto` so the sticky context is the wrapper rather than the page.\n- **Cross-origin iframe postMessage spying is blocked.** Playwright (Chromium) throws `SecurityError: Blocked a frame with origin \"...\" from accessing a cross-origin frame` if you try to replace methods on `iframe.contentWindow` for a third-party embed (YouTube, Vimeo, etc.). For seek/play verification on cross-origin video embeds, prefer (a) observable side-effects in the parent's DOM/state (e.g. our own `data-active` flips), or (b) routing the test through an HTML5 `<video>` MP4 path where `video.currentTime` IS reachable from the parent and `Object.defineProperty(v, 'currentTime', { get, set, configurable: true })` works. Also: install the descriptor override AFTER reading the post-mount value, otherwise the React `useEffect` initial assignment is lost.\n- **Adding a new media type to course assets** = extend `CONTENT_TYPES` in `src/app/api/courses/[slug]/assets/[...path]/route.ts` (one map gates both the GET Content-Type header and the PUT extension allow-list). Currently supports `.png/.jpg/.jpeg/.svg/.webp/.gif/.mp4/.webm`.\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-051\n- Updated scripts/ralph/skills/generate_lesson/SKILL.md with a new \"Step 5: Visual Illustrations\" section placed immediately after \"Step 4: Source Research\".\n- Renumbered subsequent steps: Step 5 → Step 6 (Compose), Step 6 → Step 7 (Write+Validate), Step 7 → Step 8 (Stop). Updated The Job overview to add a new step 5 entry. Back-references to Step 4 in the body remain accurate.\n- New section spells out: inline `![alt](url)` images for theory ≥300 chars; Image widget (US-050, type `image`) for hero figures; preferred sources (Wikimedia Commons / public-domain repos / official docs / arxiv); avoided hosts (medium / towardsdatascience / dev.to / personal blogs / Imgur); REQUIRED meaningful alt text; attribution format `Wikimedia Commons, <author>, <license>` + `attribution.url` to Commons file page.\n- Worked example (Median filter) bumped from 4 → 5 sections: theory now includes an inline Wikimedia salt-and-pepper noise image with descriptive alt; new image-widget section \"edge-preservation-figure\" before the quiz, with full alt + caption + Wikimedia attribution. Footer \"Why this lesson works\" updated to describe both visual additions.\n- Validation checklist appended four new items covering inline-image-on-long-theory, meaningful alt text, host avoidlist, and Wikimedia attribution format.\n- Files changed: scripts/ralph/skills/generate_lesson/SKILL.md\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` passes (clean). `npm run lint` shows 162 errors but they are all pre-existing on the branch (this PR only touches a markdown file — `git diff --name-only HEAD` confirms).\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - When a skill update is purely documentation (markdown only), AC almost always says \"Typecheck passes\" not \"Lint passes\" — a no-op markdown change can't move either metric. Don't get distracted trying to fix unrelated lint errors that pre-date the branch.\n  - Renumbering `## Step N` headings means hunting back-references too. `grep -n \"Step [0-9]\" SKILL.md` surfaces them all in one pass; the existing back-references in this file all referred to Step 4 (Source Research), which kept its number, so no follow-up edits were needed.\n  - The Image widget (US-050) was marked `passes: true` in prd.json but the code (src/widgets/Image/, schemas/image.json, registry entry, ImageSectionSchema variant) is NOT in the repo — the US-050 merge commit only landed the orchestrator's progress logs. The `image` section type the SKILL.md now references won't actually validate against `LessonSchema` until US-050's code lands. Worth flagging for the next reviewer / for whoever picks up the next image-needing story.\n  - Worked example URLs: I used realistic-looking Wikimedia Commons URLs (`upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/...`) for both the inline image (Noise_salt_and_pepper.png) and the Image widget (Median_filter_step_edge.png). They follow the canonical Wikimedia URL shape but I did not live-verify the exact filenames exist — the worked example is documentation, not a live asset; if a future agent copies the URLs into a real lesson they should re-verify on Commons.\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-052\n- Added `src/components/LessonChat.tsx` — sliding side-panel scaffold with header (AI Tutor + X close), scrollable message list with empty-state placeholder, sticky footer composer (autoresize textarea max 6 lines, Stop placeholder, Send button). ⌘+Enter sends; plain Enter inserts newline; Send disabled when textarea is empty/whitespace-only. Send appends a user bubble + canned assistant placeholder bubble (\"Backend not connected — try again after US-053 ships\") and clears the draft.\n- Draft persisted to `localStorage['lessonChatDraft:<courseSlug>:<lessonSlug>']` debounced 200ms via `setTimeout` inside a `useEffect`. Restored on mount/slug change. Cleared on Send.\n- Panel open/closed state persisted to `localStorage['lessonChat-open']`. Hydrated from storage on mount in the same effect that already hydrates `toc-collapsed`.\n- Lesson page wiring: `chatOpen` state is now stateful (was `useState(false)` const-only); ⌘. keyboard shortcut and the toolbar tutor button (`tutor-btn`) both call `toggleChat` which flips state + persists. `closeChat` (used by panel X button) sets `false` + persists.\n- Toolbar tutor button gains an `active` prop on `ToolbarIconBtn`: when on, paints `--accent-subtle` background + `--accent-text`; sets `aria-pressed=\"true\"` and `data-active=\"true\"`. Old `disabled` styling preserved for other buttons.\n- Removed the placeholder `ChatColumn` (\"AI tutor — coming soon\") component — no longer referenced.\n- Animation: existing `transition: 'grid-template-columns 180ms ease, opacity 600ms ease'` on the lesson shell already drives the slide (chatCol toggles 0px ↔ 320px). `prefers-reduced-motion` is honoured globally via `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { *, *::before, *::after { transition-duration: 0.01ms !important; ... } }` in `src/app/globals.css` — covers both the grid-column transition and any inline transitions we'd add.\n- Files changed: src/app/courses/[slug]/lessons/[lessonSlug]/page.tsx (Toolbar wiring, ⌘. shortcut, ChatColumn removed), src/components/LessonChat.tsx (new).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` shows the same 2 pre-existing pyodide test errors as before US-052 (unchanged baseline). `npm run lint` matches baseline 910/2318 (no new errors or warnings). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-052.js`, 23 assertions) passes — covers panel default-closed, tutor-btn enabled, ⌘. open/close, draft persistence + restoration after reload, whitespace-only Send disabled, ⌘+Enter sends, plain Enter newline, X button persists closed state, no console errors. Screenshots at /tmp/US-052-after-send.png and /tmp/US-052-final.png confirm chrome.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - `display: 'none'` panels are not \"visible\" to playwright's default `waitForSelector`. When asserting a panel returned to closed state, use `page.waitForFunction(() => el.getAttribute('data-open') === 'false')` or pass `state: 'attached'` — otherwise the wait times out even though the attribute already flipped (13× resolved-to-hidden in the timeout log is the tell).\n  - The `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect` lint rule fires on `setX(dynamicValue)` inside a `useEffect` body but NOT on `setX(literalValue)`. So an `if (stored) setX(stored); else setX('')` pattern triggers the rule on the first branch (dynamic) — but only one disable directive is needed. If you collapse to `setX(stored ?? '')` the rule still fires because the value is dynamic; keep the inline-disable comment.\n  - Existing prefers-reduced-motion handling is global via `globals.css` — there's no `usePrefersReducedMotion` hook in the repo and we don't need one for this story. Inline `transition: ...` styles ARE overridden by the global `transition-duration: 0.01ms !important` rule, so JS-side gating isn't required.\n  - `ToolbarIconBtn`'s old style mixed `border: '1px solid transparent'` shorthand with conditional values. Codebase pattern (per progress.txt note) is to use longhand `borderWidth`/`borderStyle`/`borderColor` so hover/active variants only override the longhand piece — applied here too.\n  - When wiring localStorage hydration on a lesson page, prefer adding to the existing single mount-effect that already hydrates other persisted state (TOC collapse), rather than spawning a second one. Keeps SSR-flash window down to one frame for both keys.\n  - The previous attempt at US-052 (per `scripts/ralph/logs/US-052.last_failure.txt`) implemented the same code, all checks passed, but the agent exited without `git commit` — losing the entire diff. The orchestrator's \"0 commits\" guard caught it. Always commit BEFORE celebrating green tests.\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-053\n- Added `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts` — server-side connector module with two implementations selectable at first use and cached for the process lifetime: (a) `agentSdkConnector` dynamically `import('@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk')` and iterates the `query()` async stream, accumulating `assistant` text blocks (falls back to the final `result` field if no blocks were yielded); (b) `subprocessConnector` spawns `claude -p \"<prompt>\" --output-format json` via `child_process.spawn`, parses the JSON, and returns `parsed.result`. Selection: `selectConnector()` tries SDK first, falls back to subprocess after a `claude --version` probe; returns `null` when neither works. Subprocess connector enforces a 60s SIGTERM timeout with a 5s SIGKILL grace, both configurable for tests.\n- Added `src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts` — POST handler that validates body via Zod (`courseSlug, lessonSlug, sectionId?, message, history?`), invokes the cached connector, returns `{ assistant: string }` on success, `503` with the spec-mandated body `'AI Tutor unavailable: install Claude Code CLI or sign in to Claude Max.'` when no connector is available, and `502` when the connector itself throws.\n- Wired `src/components/LessonChat.tsx` Send button to POST `/api/lesson-chat`. While the request is in flight, a pending assistant bubble renders 3 animated dots (`<TypingDots/>`, keyframes injected via inline `<style>` since globals.css doesn't have a tutor-specific animation). On success the pending bubble swaps to the real text. On 503 (or any non-OK status, or fetch failure), the bubble swaps to a red error variant (new `errorBubbleStyle` using `--danger-*` tokens, falling back to literal hex). The Send button is also disabled while sending. History is built from prior non-pending messages and sent as `history: Array<{role, content}>` per AC, even though the stub prompt assembly ignores it (US-054).\n- Did NOT install `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` because the package's `peerDependencies` declares `zod@^4.0.0` and the codebase pins `zod@3.x` (per Codebase Patterns). The dynamic import in `agentSdkConnector` raises `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` at first use, which `selectConnector` treats as the documented \"package missing\" fallback path. Adding the package later would require either resolving the peer-dep conflict project-wide or pinning it via `--legacy-peer-deps`. Flagged for whoever ships the SDK path.\n- Files changed: src/components/LessonChat.tsx (modified — backend wiring + typing dots + error bubble); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts (new); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts (new); src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts (new); src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts (new).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 190/190 (added 23 new tests in lessonChat — subprocess parsing, non-zero exit, JSON parse error, missing result field, is_error=true, timeout via mocked spawn, spawn-throws path, SDK collects assistant blocks, SDK falls back to result, SDK no-text rejects, SDK is_error rejects, loadSdk rejects (mimics package missing); selection prefers SDK; selection falls back when SDK throws; selection returns null when both fail; selection caches across calls; route 400/200/503/502 handlers). `npm run lint` 162 errors / 24 warnings — same pre-existing baseline as US-052 (no new errors or warnings from this change). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-053.js`, 8 assertions) passes against the real `claude -p` subprocess: navigates to `/courses/gauss-basics/lessons/wprowadzenie-do-filtrowania`, opens the chat panel, types \"What is this app?\", confirms user bubble + typing dots + assistant bubble within ~30s, checks textarea is cleared, no console errors. Real assistant response excerpt: \"This app is an AI Lecturer — a single-user local web app that delivers interactive AI-generated lessons…\". Screenshots at /tmp/US-053-typing.png and /tmp/US-053-final.png.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **claude-agent-sdk has a hard zod v4 peer-dep.** Repo pins zod v3 because `zod-to-json-schema` returns `{}` on zod v4 (existing Codebase Patterns note). Dynamic-importing the SDK and treating `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` as a fallback condition keeps both paths viable without forcing a zod bump. If a future story wants the SDK path live, the cleanest fix is probably moving `zod-to-json-schema` calls behind a typed shim and then bumping zod — not `--legacy-peer-deps`, which leaves both versions in the tree and breaks the discriminator-based widget schemas.\n  - **`claude -p \"<prompt>\" --output-format json` payload shape** (verified live in this story): top-level keys include `type: \"result\"`, `subtype: \"success\"|...`, `is_error: boolean`, `result: string` (the assistant text), `duration_ms`, `usage`, `modelUsage`, `permission_denials`, `terminal_reason`. The minimum to extract assistant text is `JSON.parse(stdout).result` after asserting `is_error !== true`. Don't try to read `--output-format stream-json` or per-message blocks; for non-streaming we only need the final `result`.\n  - **Subprocess timeout pattern in node** with both SIGTERM + SIGKILL grace: outer `setTimeout` calls `child.kill('SIGTERM')` and starts an inner `setTimeout` for SIGKILL `killGraceMs` ms later. Inner timer is cleared inside `settle()` to avoid leaking when the process actually exits between SIGTERM and SIGKILL. The `close` event fires after kill regardless of which signal landed. For tests, mock `kill()` to emit the synthetic `close(143)` event back to satisfy the EventEmitter contract.\n  - **Module-level cache for connector selection.** AC says \"selection cached at server boot\" — Next.js App Router has no `onServerStart`, so module-level lazy cache (`let cached: Connector | null | undefined`) hydrated by the first call to `selectConnector()` is the idiomatic equivalent. Export a `_resetConnectorCacheForTesting()` so unit tests don't poison each other across `forceReselect: true` calls.\n  - **vi.spyOn on a re-exported barrel function** (`vi.spyOn(connectorModule, 'selectConnector')`) only intercepts calls that go through the imported symbol, not direct calls inside the module. Because the route handler imports `selectConnector` from `@/lib/lessonChat/connector` and the test imports the same module, the spy works without `vi.mock(...)`. Same pattern as the existing `api.test.ts` — keep the alias path consistent.\n  - **Playwright `run.js` leaves `.temp-execution-<ts>.js` artifacts** in `scripts/ralph/skills/playwright-skill/`. Not gitignored; show up as untracked in `git status` after a browser run. Delete them before committing.\n  - **Lesson chat history field is plumbed end-to-end but unused by the prompt assembly stub.** The route accepts `history: Array<{role, content}>` and the connector signature receives it via `ConnectorRequest`, but `assemblePrompt` only uses `message`. Wiring history (and `sectionId` lesson context) into the actual prompt is US-054. Don't drop the history-collection code in the component when working on US-054 — just teach `assemblePrompt` to consume it.\n  - **For UI tags requiring backend, run `npm run dev` then point playwright at it.** Detection helper missed the server initially (per the existing playwright skill note); just hardcode `TARGET_URL = 'http://localhost:3000/...'` in the test script. The dev server starts cleanly in <300ms with Turbopack.\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-054\n- Added `src/lib/lessonChat/context.ts` exporting `buildPromptContext({ lesson, currentSectionId? }) → { systemPrompt, contextBlock }`. `systemPrompt` is the spec-mandated string verbatim. `contextBlock` always leads with `Lesson title: …\\nEyebrow: …\\nDescription: …\\nEstimated minutes: …`. When `currentSectionId` resolves to a real section, the active section is serialised per type (theory→`markdown`; quiz→question + numbered options; code→`taskMarkdown` + `starterCode`; codeCloze→`taskMarkdown`+template; sandbox/demo/histogram/plotImage/parametricExplorer/dragMatch/dataTable/video/custom→short type-specific summary). When `currentSectionId` is omitted or unknown, falls back to `Lesson outline:\\n- [<type>] <title>` lines. If the assembled string exceeds `CONTEXT_CHAR_LIMIT` (4000) the body portion is truncated and `'… [truncated]'` appended; the lesson header is preserved.\n- Wired `src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts` to load the lesson via `lessonFile()` + `LessonSchema.parse()`, call `buildPromptContext({ lesson, currentSectionId: req.body.sectionId })`, and pass `{ systemPrompt, userMessage: '<contextBlock>\\n\\nUser question: <message>', history }` to the connector. New 404 path when the lesson file is missing; 500 when stored JSON fails schema.\n- Updated `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts` interface: `ConnectorRequest` now `{ systemPrompt?, userMessage, history? }` (was `{ message, history? }`). Both `subprocessConnector` and `agentSdkConnector` pass `assemblePrompt(req.userMessage, req.systemPrompt)` to the underlying CLI/SDK. `assemblePrompt` retains a default fallback prompt for direct callers/tests.\n- `src/components/LessonChat.tsx` now tracks the active section via IntersectionObserver. Effect runs when `open` flips true; observes every `[data-section-id]` element with `rootMargin: '0px 0px -75% 0px'` so only the strip near the top of the viewport counts as \"active\". Picks the highest-DOM-order intersecting section so when several straddle the band we keep the one the learner has scrolled into. Seeds an initial `activeSectionId` from current scroll position so the first message after opening has a value before IO fires. Wraps in a `MutationObserver` on `document.body` so sections rendered AFTER the panel opens (lesson loads async) get observed too. The fetch POST now includes `sectionId: activeSectionId ?? undefined`. Panel exposes `data-active-section-id={activeSectionId ?? ''}` for tests.\n- Added `src/lib/lessonChat/context.test.ts` (8 tests): systemPrompt verbatim, lesson metadata in contextBlock, theory-section body, quiz question+numbered options, code task+starter, outline fallback when sectionId omitted, outline fallback when sectionId unknown, truncation when content exceeds 4000 chars.\n- Updated `src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts` to write a fake lesson under a `mkdtempSync` `COURSES_ROOT_OVERRIDE` so the new disk read works; also added a \"passes systemPrompt + contextBlock-derived userMessage to connector\" test that captures the connector's `chat()` arg and checks for `Lesson title:`, the section markdown, and `User question: <text>` substrings. Plus a 404 case when the lesson file is missing.\n- Updated existing `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts` to use the new `userMessage` field in place of `message`.\n- Files changed: src/lib/lessonChat/context.ts (new); src/lib/lessonChat/context.test.ts (new); src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts (modified — fetches lesson, builds context); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts (modified — ConnectorRequest shape); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts (modified — userMessage); src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts (modified — disk fixture + new assertions); src/components/LessonChat.tsx (modified — IO + MO + sectionId in POST).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 200/200 (added 9 new tests across context.test.ts and route.test.ts). `npm run lint` baseline 163/25 unchanged from US-053. Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-054.js`, 8 assertions) passes against the real `claude -p` subprocess: navigates to seed lesson, opens chat panel, programmatically scrolls main content so quiz section `s2-quiz-kernel` reaches the top, waits for `data-active-section-id` to flip to that id, sends \"What is this question testing?\", confirms POST body includes `sectionId: 's2-quiz-kernel'`, and validates the assistant reply explicitly references the **kernel** quiz topic (\"This question is testing whether you understand the **definition of a kernel** in image filtering — specifically, that it's a small matrix of weights used in the convolution operation.\"). Screenshot at /tmp/US-054-after-send.png.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **IntersectionObserver vs late-rendering targets.** When the chat panel opens immediately after navigation, the `useEffect` may run before the lesson page has fetched its data and rendered `[data-section-id]` elements. A first-pass `document.querySelectorAll('[data-section-id]')` returns 0. The fix is twofold: (a) observe what's currently mounted, (b) wrap a `MutationObserver` on `document.body` (`{childList: true, subtree: true}`) and re-call `observeAllSections()` on each mutation, with a `WeakSet<Element>` to avoid double-observing. The old \"queryAll once in useEffect\" pattern only works when the parent's content is statically rendered before the effect runs.\n  - **rootMargin trick for \"section-at-the-top\" detection.** `rootMargin: '0px 0px -75% 0px'` shrinks the IntersectionObserver root rect so only the top 25% of the viewport counts as \"intersecting\". Combined with picking the highest-DOM-order intersecting section, you reliably get \"what is the user reading right now\". A naive ratio-based pick gets fooled by big sections (a long theory section with ratio 0.6 wins over a small quiz with ratio 1.0).\n  - **vitest + COURSES_ROOT_OVERRIDE for route handlers that touch fs.** The lesson-chat route now reads from disk. To test it without polluting `courses/`, snapshot the env var, point it at a `mkdtempSync(...)` dir, write a single fake lesson JSON inside `<root>/<courseSlug>/lessons/<lessonSlug>.json`, and restore the env var in `afterAll`. `assertSafeSlug` in `paths.ts` is happy with the slugs `c` / `l` so the fixture file path is just `<tmp>/c/lessons/l.json`.\n  - **Connector signature is an internal contract — break it freely between US-053 and US-054.** Renaming `ConnectorRequest.message → userMessage` and adding `systemPrompt?` was the cleanest expression of \"the route now owns prompt assembly\". Updating the existing test fixture's `message: 'hi'` → `userMessage: 'hi'` was a one-pass `sed`. Don't try to keep both shapes for backwards compatibility — the connector has exactly one consumer (the API route) so the bridge is invisible.\n  - **Playwright skill `run.js` requires the script to self-wrap.** A `module.exports = async ({ chromium }) => {...}` pattern silently no-ops because the wrapper only auto-wraps inline code; for file mode it just `require()`s the file and assumes the script self-executes. Use the existing US-053 pattern: `const { chromium } = require('playwright'); (async () => { ... })().catch(...)`. The wrapper documents this in the auto-wrap branch only — a script that lacks the `(async () => {` token but has `require(` is run as-is.\n  - **playwright `waitForSelector('...[data-open=\"true\"]')` doesn't poll attribute changes well.** Even when the attribute is set in the same render commit as the element appearing, the visibility check sometimes flakes. Prefer `page.waitForFunction(() => el.getAttribute('data-open') === 'true')` for attribute transitions — same pattern as the US-052 close-state assertion learning.\n  - **Test cleanup matters before browser runs in dev.** localStorage state from the prior US-053 run (`lessonChat-open`, `lessonChatDraft:…`) survives across reloads and test runs because the dev server points at the same origin. The US-054 playwright test removes both keys in-page before the explicit reload, otherwise the chat panel opens unexpectedly and the `tutor-btn` toggle inverts.\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-055\n- Added `src/lib/lessonChat/inflight.ts` — module-level `Map<requestId, AbortController>` registry plus `register/unregister/abortInflight` helpers and test utilities. Streaming POST registers an entry; the matching DELETE looks it up and calls `.abort()`. Entries are removed on stream end (success, error, or client-disconnect via the ReadableStream's `cancel` callback) so the map can't leak.\n- Extended `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts` `Connector` interface with `chatStream(req, signal): AsyncIterable<ChatStreamEvent>` (event types: `token{text}`, `done`, `error{message}`). Subprocess implementation spawns `claude -p \"<prompt>\"` (NO `--output-format json` — raw streaming text) and emits each newline-terminated stdout line as a `token` event; on close emits `done` (or `error` for non-zero exit). On signal abort: SIGTERM → SIGKILL after `killGraceMs` and the generator returns without `done` so the route can emit a final `aborted` SSE event. SDK implementation iterates `sdk.query({prompt})` and emits each `assistant` content text block as a `token` event; falls back to the `result` field if no blocks were yielded; aborts cooperatively when `signal.aborted` flips.\n- Extended `src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts` POST to inspect `Accept: text/event-stream`. When present, returns a `ReadableStream` that: (1) sends the spec-mandated FIRST event `event: meta\\ndata: {\"requestId\":\"<uuid>\"}\\n\\n` so the client knows what to DELETE, (2) iterates `connector.chatStream(args, abortController.signal)` and re-emits `token`/`done`/`error` events, (3) on signal abort emits a final `aborted` event before closing. The non-SSE JSON path is preserved as-is for the non-streaming consumers (existing tests). The `extract+resolve` lesson-loading path was hoisted into a `resolveRequest` helper so both code paths share validation/file-loading logic without duplication.\n- Added `src/app/api/lesson-chat/[requestId]/route.ts` — DELETE handler that calls `abortInflight(requestId)`. Returns 204 on success, 404 if no inflight entry was found.\n- Updated `src/components/LessonChat.tsx` Send flow to fetch with `Accept: text/event-stream`, read the body via `getReader()` + `TextDecoder`, parse SSE blocks (split on `\\n\\n`, then per-line `event:` / `data:` parsing), and:\n  - Track per-request `AbortController` (`streamAbortRef`) and `requestId` (`currentRequestIdRef`).\n  - On each `token` event, append `obj.text` to a local `accumulated` string and `setMessages` so the assistant bubble updates in real time.\n  - On Stop click: flip a `userStoppedRef`, call `abortController.abort()` (kills the fetch reader), and best-effort `DELETE /api/lesson-chat/<requestId>` (server-side cancel; failures swallowed because the client-side abort already finalised the bubble).\n  - On stream end (or abort with `userStoppedRef.current === true`): set the bubble's `stopped: true` flag and freeze its text to `accumulated`. The bubble renders the partial text + a `<span>` with `STOPPED_SUFFIX = \"— stopped by user\"` styled `fontStyle: italic; color: var(--text-tertiary); display: block` (its own line).\n  - Stop button is now `disabled={!sending}` with an active-state style (border-strong + bg-base + text-base, cursor: pointer); pre-existing `stopBtnStyle` retained for the disabled state.\n  - TypingDots no longer embed a `<style>` keyframes tag inside the bubble; the keyframes are hoisted to a single `<style>` directly inside `<aside>` so they don't pollute `textContent` when tests read the bubble.\n- Added/extended tests:\n  - `src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts`: 5 new tests — `subprocessConnector.chatStream` line-by-line tokenisation + `done`, abort kills child without `done`, non-zero exit → `error`; `agentSdkConnector.chatStream` token + done, abort stops mid-stream.\n  - `src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts`: 4 new tests — SSE returns 200 + `text/event-stream` content-type + meta+token+done events; SSE registers requestId and DELETE aborts the in-flight signal (verifies `_inflightSizeForTesting()` returns 0 after); DELETE 404 for unknown requestId; SSE 503 fallback when no connector. Helper `fakeConnector(chatImpl, streamImpl?)` produces a typed `Connector` for spies.\n- Files changed: src/lib/lessonChat/connector.ts (chatStream impls); src/lib/lessonChat/inflight.ts (new); src/app/api/lesson-chat/route.ts (SSE branch + resolveRequest helper); src/app/api/lesson-chat/[requestId]/route.ts (new); src/components/LessonChat.tsx (streaming + Stop wiring + suffix styling); src/lib/lessonChat/connector.test.ts (5 new tests); src/lib/lessonChat/route.test.ts (4 new tests + fakeConnector helper).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 209/209 (added 9 tests; no existing tests changed in behaviour, only mock shapes adapted to the new interface). `npm run lint` 162 errors / 24 warnings (= US-054 baseline 163/25 minus 1 of each — all changes are clean; the dropped pair is an `Unused eslint-disable` directive that no longer fires because adjacent code shifted). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-055.js`, 12 assertions) passes against the real `claude -p` subprocess: panel opens, Stop initially disabled, partial text streams in (1095 chars before stop), Stop becomes enabled mid-stream, click Stop → bubble flips to `data-stopped=\"true\"` with the partial text PLUS the italic suffix on its own line (1113 chars total, suffix is `display: block; font-style: italic`), Stop disabled after stream ended, follow-up question replies normally (\"2 + 2 = 4. …\"). Screenshot at /tmp/US-055-after-stop.png.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **Inline `<style>` tags inside repeating React subtrees pollute `textContent`.** TypingDots originally rendered `<style>{keyframes}</style>` inside its own span. The CSS string (~140 chars) was visible to `el.textContent` reads — making playwright's \"wait until bubble has > N chars\" check pass against the keyframes, NOT actual streamed tokens. Hoist single-instance keyframes to the panel root (or a non-text region) so element textContent reflects ONLY the user-visible string. Same trap applies to `<script>` tags.\n  - **`claude -p \"<prompt>\"` (no `--output-format`) streams raw assistant text to stdout.** Each newline-terminated chunk is one paragraph/code-fence/list-line; total stream completes on process exit. For SSE we read line-by-line via `child.stdout.on('data', ...)` + buffer-and-split, NOT via `--output-format stream-json` (which emits per-message JSON envelopes and complicates parsing for what is essentially a passthrough).\n  - **Two-channel cancel: client AbortController + server DELETE.** The Stop button does BOTH. `ac.abort()` on the client ends fetch reading immediately so the bubble freezes. `DELETE /api/lesson-chat/<requestId>` reaches across and kills the subprocess (or aborts the SDK iterator). Without DELETE, the server keeps the subprocess alive draining stdout into a closed stream until the OS reclaims it. Without ac.abort(), the client keeps draining and might process tokens that arrive AFTER the user clicked Stop. Both are necessary; neither alone is correct.\n  - **Map<requestId, AbortController> in module scope IS the canonical \"in-flight registry\" for Next.js App Router.** No global server context exists; module-level state is the equivalent of a process-wide singleton (it's per-route-handler-module but Node only loads each module once per dev/prod server). Always wire a `_resetInflightForTesting()` so tests are deterministic — vitest workers share the module too.\n  - **AbortController wired through ReadableStream's `cancel` callback handles client disconnect.** When the client closes the SSE connection (browser navigation, fetch abort, network drop), the runtime invokes `cancel()` on the ReadableStream. We call `abortController.abort()` + `unregisterInflight(requestId)` from there. This catches the case where the client never sent DELETE (e.g. tab close, Wi-Fi drop) — no leaked subprocess.\n  - **SSE message frame parsing in TypeScript.** Frames are separated by `\\n\\n`. Each frame has lines starting with `event:` / `data:` / `id:` / `retry:` / `:` (comment). For our use, we only need `event:` and `data:`. Multiple `data:` lines in the same frame are joined with `\\n` (per spec). Always `decoder.decode(value, { stream: true })` so multi-byte UTF-8 spanning chunks doesn't break, then a final `decoder.decode()` after the loop to flush the last partial sequence. Same pattern any token-streaming UI in this repo can adopt.\n  - **`accumulated` closure-variable pattern for streaming setState.** Rather than reading-then-writing React state in each token handler, keep a local `let accumulated = ''` in the async closure, increment it sync, and call `setMessages(prev => ...)` with a snapshot. Reading state inside an async callback would race against React's batched updates; the local var is the source of truth for \"what tokens have we received so far\" and gets passed to `finalizeStopped`/the post-loop terminal `setMessages` once.\n  - **Typed `Connector` interface gotcha for `vi.spyOn` mocks.** Adding `chatStream` to `Connector` broke every existing spy that returned `{ name, chat }`. Test helper `fakeConnector(chatImpl, streamImpl?)` papers over this by defaulting `chatStream` to a single-token-then-done generator built from `chat`. Adopt a similar helper any time the Connector contract grows so the existing spies stay one-line.\n  - **Don't put debug `console.log` near token streaming in playwright tests UNLESS you also ingest them in the test.** I added `[chat-debug]` lines in the component, then teaches the playwright `page.on('console')` listener to surface them — that's how I noticed the TypingDots-textContent bug (catch path ran with `accumulated.length=0` even though the bubble seemed to have 142 chars of content).\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-056\n- Authored `test-plan.md` at the repo root: end-to-end QA walkthrough that a maintainer can run in ≤30 min (experienced) / ≤60 min (new). Structure: §1 Setup → §2 Test Courses to Generate (3 distinct topics chosen to exercise different widget mixes — Course A \"Bubble sort algorithm\" Short/Beginner/Mostly-theory, Course B \"Linear regression for ML\" Standard/Some-experience/Balanced, Course C \"Computer vision: convolutions, edges, and Fourier transforms\" Comprehensive/Advanced/Mostly-practice) → §3 Walkthroughs (numbered checklist sections per area) → §4 Known limitations → §5 Sign-off.\n- Each test course entry lists topic prompt for Stage 1, exact refinement choices (level + duration chip + slider position with label), expected approximate module/lesson count tied to US-042's enum, and which widgets each course is expected to exercise.\n- Walkthrough sections (§3.1–§3.9) cover all the AC items: dashboard (empty + populated + Continue learning hero + search substring + slug-prefix + autocomplete) [US-014/019/036], wizard end-to-end Stages 1–5 [US-024/025/026/042], lesson layout + nav + TOC + prev/next + mark complete + auto-advance + j/k/⌘B/⌘. + Reset session [US-015/016/017/018/019/021/031], every widget type 14 sub-sections [US-007/008/010/011/012/013/030/038/039/043/044/045/046/047/048/049/050/051], LessonChat open + ask with section context + stop mid-stream + draft persistence + 503 fallback [US-052/053/054/055], theme toggle persistence [US-002/020], sources panel lesson + section [US-040/041], schemaVersion [US-037], logo navigates home [US-034], avatar dropdown [US-035].\n- Each item is a checkbox `- [ ]` with an explicit pass criterion (e.g. \"button morphs to **Submitted ✓**\", \"card badge shows **In progress · 1/N**\", \"**PNG appears within 10 s** of first matplotlib install\", \"italic suffix `— stopped by user` renders on its own line\", \"iframe src appends `?start=<sec>`\"). No vague \"works correctly\".\n- §4 Known Limitations lists 14 explicit out-of-MVP items: single-user, file-based not IndexedDB, course generation runs offline via ralph CLI not in-app, LessonChat needs Claude CLI, no cross-reload chat history, **PythonPlot (US-044) and Image (US-050) may not be wired in the registry** (per US-051 progress note: marked `passes: true` but code missing — the §3.4.8 PythonPlot and §3.4.14 Image sections include explicit \"verify with grep, mark N/A if absent\" instructions, so reviewers don't get stuck), Pyodide cooperative-interrupt impossible, in-memory wizard draft, search-index staleness on theory edit, widget-data search exclusion, no Polish accent-stripping, light-mode default, agent log out of scope, schemaVersion forward-compat only.\n- Files changed: test-plan.md (new — only file added; no code changes).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean (this is the only AC quality gate — `tests pass` and `lint passes` are not in the AC for US-056). No code touched, so no lint or test delta possible.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **Pure-documentation stories DO need to verify the story's specific quality gate, but only that one.** US-056's AC says \"Typecheck passes\" — not \"tests pass\", not \"lint clean\". Don't waste time on lint/test runs when the AC doesn't require them; just verify the one named gate. Same lesson as US-051 (skill-md-only stories).\n  - **`prd.json` lies about US-044 (PythonPlot) and US-050 (Image) — both marked `passes: true`, neither is actually in the registry or schema discriminated union.** Cross-checked: `src/widgets/registry.ts` `WidgetType` union has 13 types but excludes `pythonPlot` and `image`; `src/lib/schemas/lesson.ts` discriminated union likewise excludes them. The US-051 learning note flagged Image; PythonPlot has the same fate. Test plan needed to acknowledge this honestly without dropping the AC-mandated coverage — solution was to keep §3.4.8 / §3.4.14 sections complete with their pass criteria but front-load a **NOTE** at the bottom + a Known-Limitations item §4.6 with a one-line `grep` to verify.\n  - **Test plans should reference all the seed-test courses already in `/courses/`** — `pexp-test`, `cloze-test`, `dragmatch-test`, `datatable-test`, `video-test` cover the widgets that don't fit naturally into the seed `gauss-basics` course. Calling these out per-widget gives the reviewer a fast path even if they skip §2's \"Test Courses to Generate\" step (which requires a full ralph chain run).\n  - **Time-budget signal in the document.** AC says ≤30/≤60 min targets — the way I made this realistic was: (a) §2 explicitly tells reviewers they can skip course generation and use seeds (saves ~30 min × 3 courses), (b) §3 sections are ordered so the reviewer can do dashboard → wizard → seed-lesson nav → widgets → chat → theme → sources → schema → header in roughly that flow without backtracking, (c) widget §3.4 sub-sections are short enough (5–10 boxes each) that the whole §3.4 is ~10 minutes if no failures.\n  - **`courses/` already has six dedicated widget-test courses.** `ls courses/` showed cloze-test, datatable-test, dragmatch-test, gauss-basics, pexp-test, video-test — handy reference for any future story that needs a minimal repro lesson for a specific widget without spinning up the wizard.\n  - **The keyboard shortcut list in `src/components/ShortcutsModal.tsx` is the canonical source of truth** for what keys are wired. AC mentions j/k/⌘B/⌘. but ShortcutsModal.tsx also exposes Space (mark current section), n (next lesson), p (previous lesson), f (focus mode), and arrow keys ↑/↓ as aliases for k/j. Test plan covers j/k/⌘B/⌘. + Reset session per AC; the others aren't mandated so I didn't bloat the checklist.\n  - **Authoring tip: pick widget pass criteria from the original AC string.** Each `US-NNN` AC in `prd.json` has phrases like \"button morphs to **Stop** with `--danger` styling\", \"**Page X of Y** indicator centered below table\", \"italic suffix `— stopped by user` renders on its own line\". Quoting these verbatim in the checkbox items both (a) anchors the criterion to the originating story and (b) means a future reviewer can `git blame`/grep back to the spec source if a behavior changes.\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-057\n- Bug: `QuizWidget` colored options post-submit purely by `isCorrect`, so an unselected correct option still rendered green and shipped a `<Check>` icon — revealing the answer key when the learner submitted a wrong-but-non-empty subset of a multi-select quiz.\n- Extracted three pure helpers to `src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.ts`: `getOptionState({submitted,isSelected,isCorrect,multiSelect})` returning the discriminated `'idle'|'selected'|'correct'|'incorrect'|'dimmed'` state used both for `data-state` and styling; `shouldShowCheck` / `shouldShowX` for the trailing icons. Multi-select branch returns `'dimmed'` for any unselected option, regardless of correctness — single-choice branch is unchanged (`isCorrect → 'correct'` first), satisfying the AC's \"Single-choice quiz behavior is unchanged\".\n- Added `src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.test.ts` — 16 tests covering pre-submit (idle/selected for both modes), multi-select post-submit (selected+correct→correct, selected+incorrect→incorrect, **unselected+correct→dimmed [bug repro]**, unselected+incorrect→dimmed), single-choice post-submit unchanged, and the icon-visibility rules. Run against the un-fixed `getOptionState` (with `if (isCorrect) return 'correct'` first), the bug-repro test fails — that's the \"failing test reproducing the bug\" gate.\n- `QuizWidget.tsx` now derives state once per option via the helper, threads it into the (also-refactored) `optionStyle` / `optionTextStyle` / `optionLetterStyle` builders that switch on `OptionState` instead of independent `isSelected/isCorrect` reads, and emits the same data-state on the `<button>`. `data-quiz-option`, `aria-checked`, role=`checkbox`/`radio`, Try-again reset, hover handling, and the explanation panel are all untouched.\n- Files changed: src/widgets/Quiz/QuizWidget.tsx (modified — refactored to consume helpers); src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.ts (new); src/widgets/Quiz/optionState.test.ts (new).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 225/225 (was 209 → +16 new). `npm run lint` 163 errors / 24 warnings — identical to `git stash` baseline of HEAD~ (no regression from this change). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-057.js`, 28 assertions) passes against `/courses/gauss-basics/lessons/filtr-gaussa-w-praktyce` (multi-select quiz `s4-quiz-properties`, correct=[0,1,2]); test selects option 0 (correct) + option 3 (incorrect) and asserts: 0→correct (selected+green ✓ icon, bg=success-subtle #e3f3ec), 1/2→dimmed (unselected correct, neutral bg=bg-elevated, NO icon), 3→incorrect (selected+red ✗ icon, bg=danger-subtle), 4→dimmed; Try-again resets all to idle; visit single-choice quiz `s2-quiz-kernel` confirms `role=radio` and exactly 1 option still ends up `data-state=correct` (single-choice unchanged); no console errors. Screenshot at `/tmp/US-057-quiz.png`.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **Quiz post-submit color logic now goes through a single pure helper.** Future \"show feedback after submit\" widget changes (Quiz, but also future MCQ-style widgets) should add new states to `OptionState` and switch in the style builders — don't re-derive from `isSelected/isCorrect` flags inline. The bug here was exactly that: two branches independently checked `isCorrect` first and forgot the `multiSelect && !isSelected` guard.\n  - **`vitest` runs in node env (no JSDOM, no `@testing-library`)** — confirmed by `vitest.config.ts: environment: 'node'`, no `jsdom` / `@testing-library/*` in `package.json`. To unit-test React widget logic, extract pure decision functions (`getOptionState`, `shouldShowCheck` etc.) into a sibling `.ts` file and test those, then have the React component consume the helpers. Don't try to render with `react-dom/server` or stand up jsdom — neither is wired in.\n  - **`getComputedStyle(...).backgroundColor` over a CSS var is flaky at deviceScaleFactor=1 in headless Chromium.** Setting up the page with `deviceScaleFactor: 2` returned the exact `rgb(227, 243, 236)` (= `#e3f3ec` = `--success-subtle`); without it, Chromium reported a slightly drifted `rgb(238, 244, 251)` even though the inline `style` attribute and `--success-subtle` CSS var both resolved correctly via `el.getAttribute('style')` and `getComputedStyle(documentElement).getPropertyValue('--success-subtle')`. For visual assertions in playwright tests, prefer (a) checking the `data-state` attribute (deterministic), (b) using `deviceScaleFactor: 2` for precise computed-style rgb(), or (c) screenshot diffing.\n  - **Bug-fix story TDD pattern.** AC said \"A failing test reproducing the bug exists before the fix\" — the cleanest expression is: extract the broken decision into a pure helper, write tests that describe the FIXED behavior (one of which specifically captures the bug case, e.g. `unselected+correct → dimmed`), then implement the helper to pass. Anyone running those tests against a reverted helper ('isCorrect first' branch order) sees the bug-repro test fail. Don't need a separate \"broken first, fixed second\" commit.\n  - **CSS tokens — `--success-subtle / --success-border / --danger-subtle / --danger-border` exist for both light + dark themes** (lines 37-46 light, 111-121 dark in `src/styles/tokens.css`). When adding a new feedback state, use these instead of literal hex; they pre-flip on dark theme via `:root[data-theme=\"dark\"]`.\n  - **`claude/design/source/` files trip eslint** (`'CodeBlock' / 'QuizOption' is not defined react/jsx-no-undef`) — they're inspirational JSX with no imports. They've been in the lint baseline since the design folder was added; do NOT try to \"fix\" them unless you're modifying the design folder for a specific story. The test plan (US-056 §4) and CLAUDE.md root note both flag `claude/design/` as inspo, not code.\n---\n\n## 2026-05-04 - US-058\n- Bug: post-blur ✓/✗ feedback in `CodeClozeWidget` rendered icons inline (correct) BUT showed the slot.hint inside a `display: 'block'` `<span>` BELOW the input, instead of as an end-of-line comment matching the lesson's source language. Fixed by replacing the block hint span with line-level inline comment spans rendered at the end of each `<pre>` line that contains an incorrect slot.\n- Extracted four pure helpers to `src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.ts`: `commentPrefix(language)` → `'#'` for python (default) / `'//'` for js/ts; `findExistingCommentStart(line, prefix)` → string-aware search that ignores `#` / `//` inside `'..'` or `\"..\"` literals; `formatFeedbackComment(hints, prefix, hasExisting)` → returns `\"  # hint1; hint2\"` (no existing comment) or `\"; hint1; hint2\"` (append to existing comment, no new prefix); `groupSegmentsIntoLines(segments)` → splits the existing `parseTemplate` output on newlines so each rendered `<pre>` line owns its own slots + feedback span. Added `lineHasExistingComment(lineSegments, prefix)` thin wrapper that re-stringifies a line (slots stand in as `__SLOT__`) and runs `findExistingCommentStart`.\n- Added `src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.test.ts` — 23 tests across the five helpers. Bug-repro tests: `formatFeedbackComment(['Use sum'], '#', false)` must return `\"  # Use sum\"` (would fail against the old block-style hint behaviour, which never produced a comment string at all); `formatFeedbackComment(['Use sum'], '#', true)` must return `\"; Use sum\"` (covers AC: \"When the line already has a comment, the feedback comment is appended after it without breaking syntax highlighting\"). Also covers js `//` prefix, string-aware comment detection, multi-hint join with `; `, empty/whitespace filter, slot-line grouping, mid-line slots.\n- Schema: added optional `language: 'python' | 'javascript' | 'typescript'` to `CodeClozeDataSchema` (defaults to python at the helper level — existing courses without `language` keep `#` prefix). Re-ran `npm run build:schemas` to regenerate `src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json` + `lesson.json`.\n- `CodeClozeWidget.tsx` changes: (a) replaced the `<pre>` segment-flat render with a `lines.map((lineSegs, lineIdx) => …)` render — each line is a `<span data-cloze-line data-line-index>` containing the line's text/slot segments followed by an optional `<span data-testid=\"cloze-line-feedback-<lineIdx>\" data-cloze-feedback>` with the formatted comment (italic, `var(--code-comment)` color); (b) `'\\n'` is emitted between lines (preserved by `<pre>` whiteSpace); (c) `SlotInput` lost its `showHint` prop and the `slotHintStyle` block-display span is gone; (d) icons gained `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for testing; (e) wrapper span around input+icon got `whiteSpace: 'nowrap'`, and `slotIconStyle` got `display: 'inline-block'` — without both, the SVG icon was wrapping below the input inside the inline-block wrapper because lucide SVGs default to `display: block` and the inherited `whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap'` from `<pre>` allowed a break after the input.\n- Files changed: src/widgets/CodeCloze/CodeClozeWidget.tsx (modified — line-based render, removed block hint, added inline-block to icon, added nowrap to wrapper); src/widgets/CodeCloze/schema.ts (modified — `language` field added); src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.ts (new); src/widgets/CodeCloze/feedbackComment.test.ts (new); src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json + lesson.json (regenerated).\n- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 248/248 (was 225 → +23 new). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-058.js`, 15 assertions) passes against `/courses/cloze-test/lessons/box-blur` (template `def box_blur(values):\\n    total = {{aggregator}}(values)\\n    return total / {{divisor}}\\n`): types `avg` into aggregator → `data-slot-state=incorrect`, ✗ icon at correct boundingBox (right of input, vertically centered, gap < 30px), feedback span text starts with `\"  # \"` and contains the slot's hint, feedback boundingBox center y matches input center y (same row, not below); types `sum` → ✓ icon inline + feedback span removed; both slots wrong → exactly 2 feedback spans, one per line, both with `# ` prefix; no console errors. Screenshots at /tmp/US-058-correct.png and /tmp/US-058-both-wrong.png.\n- **Learnings for future iterations:**\n  - **`lucide-react` icons render as `<svg>` with `display: block` by default.** Inside an `inline-block` parent that inherits `whiteSpace: pre-wrap` (e.g. anything inside `<pre style=\"whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap'\">`), the SVG forces a line break after the preceding inline element. Symptom: icon renders BELOW the input even though DOM order says \"after\". Fix needs BOTH `whiteSpace: 'nowrap'` on the wrapper AND `display: 'inline-block'` (or `'inline'`) on the icon style. Either alone is insufficient — nowrap doesn't override block-level child display, and inline-block on the icon still wraps if the wrapper allows breaks.\n  - **String-aware comment detection.** Naive `line.indexOf('#')` would falsely match `print(\"# not a comment\")`. The walking parser (track `inStr` quote char, advance past `\\\\`-escapes, only test the prefix when not inside a string) is short enough — ~20 lines — that it's not worth pulling in a tokenizer dep. Same pattern works for `//` against single-/double-quoted JS strings; we tested both. Doesn't handle Python triple-quotes or template literals — fine for cloze line scope (cloze templates are line-by-line by definition).\n  - **Line-based rendering inside `<pre>` with `whiteSpace: 'pre-wrap'`.** To inject a per-line trailing element while preserving newline-driven line breaks, render each line as a wrapper `<span>` containing the line's content + optional trailing element, then emit a literal `'\\n'` text node between lines (skip the last). The `<pre>` parent renders the `\\n` as a real line break. This sidesteps the React fragment-newline quirk where `<>{line}{'\\n'}</>` works but `[line, '\\n']` arrays sometimes don't preserve the newline properly across React reconciliation.\n  - **Optional schema field rollout.** Adding `language: z.enum([...]).optional()` to a Zod data schema is a no-op for existing fixtures: `CodeClozeDataSchema.parse(rawData)` accepts records without the field, `validateSlotValue` is unaffected, and the helper defaults to `'python'` so `commentPrefix(undefined) → '#'`. No course migration needed. Don't forget to re-run `npm run build:schemas` so `src/widgets/schemas/code-cloze.json` reflects the new optional property — the json-schema is the contract for the LLM-side course-spec generation skill.\n  - **Bug-fix story TDD pattern (revisited from US-057).** The cleanest \"failing-test-before-fix\" expression for a UI bug is: extract the broken decision into pure helpers and write tests for the FIXED behavior. Here `formatFeedbackComment` returning `\"  # hint\"` is the fixed contract; against the old widget code (which rendered a block span and never produced a comment string at all), that test would fail because the helper didn't exist yet. AC satisfied without spinning up jsdom + react-testing-library.\n  - **`display: 'block'` on a hint span is the actual bug.** The README-level lesson here: when a parent renders an inline element trailing some other inline content, NEVER set the trailing element's display to `block` thinking \"it'll just stack below\" — yes it stacks, but it also disrupts inline-block parent sizing. If you want a line-break before the element, prefer a literal `'\\n'` text node between siblings (under a `<pre>` ancestor) or a flex column container — but in this case, the entire premise was wrong: the hint should NOT be on a separate line, it should be an inline comment.\n  - **Existing icons in `CodeClozeWidget` lacked test ids.** Added `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for browser-test access. Generally: ANY decorative icon that has different states based on user input deserves a `data-testid` + `data-state` (or `data-icon`) attribute pair, even if no test currently uses it — bug-fix stories (this one) will need it within the lifetime of the codebase.\n---\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":198,"oldLines":3,"newStart":198,"newLines":26,"lines":["   - **`display: 'block'` on a hint span is the actual bug.** The README-level lesson here: when a parent renders an inline element trailing some other inline content, NEVER set the trailing element's display to `block` thinking \"it'll just stack below\" — yes it stacks, but it also disrupts inline-block parent sizing. If you want a line-break before the element, prefer a literal `'\\n'` text node between siblings (under a `<pre>` ancestor) or a flex column container — but in this case, the entire premise was wrong: the hint should NOT be on a separate line, it should be an inline comment.","   - **Existing icons in `CodeClozeWidget` lacked test ids.** Added `data-testid=\"cloze-slot-icon-<id>\"` + `data-icon=\"correct|incorrect\"` for browser-test access. Generally: ANY decorative icon that has different states based on user input deserves a `data-testid` + `data-state` (or `data-icon`) attribute pair, even if no test currently uses it — bug-fix stories (this one) will need it within the lifetime of the codebase."," ---","+","+## 2026-05-04 - US-059","+- Bug: `ShortcutsModal` and the toolbar tutor button + chat composer hint hard-coded macOS glyphs (`⌘`, `⌥`, `⌃`, `⇧`) regardless of platform — Windows/Linux learners saw symbols their keyboards don't have. The keybind handler side was actually already correct: `isMod` in `src/lib/hooks/useKeyboardShortcuts.ts` accepts BOTH `metaKey` and `ctrlKey`, so `Mod-B` / `Mod-.` already fire for both Cmd on macOS and Ctrl on Windows/Linux. Bug was purely on the display side, plus there was zero test coverage proving `isMod` accepts ctrlKey.","+- Added `src/lib/platform/platform.ts` — pure helpers: `isMacPlatform(userAgent?)` (regex `/Mac|iPhone|iPad|iPod/i` against `navigator.userAgent`, defaults to non-mac when ua unavailable so SSR is safe), `KeyToken` type (`'mod'|'alt'|'shift'|'ctrl'|'enter'|'tab'|'space'|'esc'|'up'|'down'|'left'|'right'` plus literals), `keyLabel(token, isMac)` returns the platform-correct label (mac: `⌘/⌥/⇧/⌃/↵`; non-mac: `Ctrl/Alt/Shift/Ctrl/↵` — `↵` kept on both since it's a universal Enter glyph), `modLabel(isMac)` for inline hints (`⌘` vs `Ctrl`).","+- Added `src/lib/platform/useIsMacPlatform.ts` — SSR-safe React hook. Returns `false` on first render (matches SSR + initial hydration) then flips after `useEffect` reads `navigator.userAgent`. The non-mac default is correct for the majority of users; macOS users see one frame of \"Ctrl\" before swap. Uses an `eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/set-state-in-effect` directive for the canonical one-shot detection pattern.","+- Added `src/lib/platform/platform.test.ts` — 18 unit tests. Bug-repro group \"keyLabel — Windows / Linux (bug repro for US-059)\" asserts `keyLabel('mod', false) === 'Ctrl'` AND `!== '⌘'` for each of mod/alt/shift/ctrl. Run against a hypothetical \"always return mac glyph\" implementation, all four assertions fail — that's the failing-test-before-fix gate. Plus macOS ua detection (Macintosh + iPhone/iPad/iPod), Windows + Linux + Android negative cases, empty ua → false, all the literal pass-through cases.","+- Added `src/lib/hooks/useKeyboardShortcuts.test.ts` — 4 tests for `isMod`. Bug-repro: \"returns true when ctrlKey is held (Windows / Linux Ctrl)\" — a pre-fix Cmd-only implementation would fail. Plus metaKey, neither, both.","+- `src/components/ShortcutsModal.tsx` — `ShortcutSpec.keys` typed as `KeyToken[]` (was `string[]`); GROUPS uses semantic tokens (`'mod'`, `'shift'`, `'esc'`, `'space'`, `'enter'`, `'up'`, `'down'`, `'tab'`) instead of literal glyphs. Modal calls `useIsMacPlatform()` and threads `isMac` through `Group` → `Row` → `keyLabel` so each `<Kbd>` gets the platform-appropriate label. Modal root gains `data-platform=\"mac\"|\"non-mac\"` for browser-test assertion. Existing `data-testid=\"kbd\"` is preserved.","+- `src/components/LessonChat.tsx` — chat composer hint changed from hard-coded `'⌘+Enter to send'` to `<span data-testid=\"lesson-chat-send-hint\">{modLabel(isMac)}+Enter to send</span>`. The `isMod` predicate inside `handleKeyDown` (`e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey`) was already correct cross-platform; not changed.","+- `src/app/courses/[slug]/lessons/[lessonSlug]/page.tsx` — extracted the inline `⌘.` toolbar glyph into a new `TutorToolbarButton` subcomponent that calls `useIsMacPlatform()` and renders `⌘.` on mac vs `Ctrl+.` on Windows/Linux (added `+` separator on non-mac to match Windows/Linux convention; on mac the bare `⌘.` keeps Apple's no-separator convention). Glyph wrapper gets `data-testid=\"tutor-btn-shortcut\"`. The keyboard-shortcut handlers in this page (`Mod-B`, `Mod-.`, j/k/↑/↓, etc.) all use `isMod(e)` which already accepts ctrlKey — no logic change needed.","+- `src/app/test-code-runner/page.tsx` — also updated the dev-test-page title from `\"Try it: print('hi'), then ⌘+Enter\"` to template-literal `${modLabel(isMac)}+Enter` for consistency. Page is dev-only.","+- Files changed: src/lib/platform/platform.ts (new); src/lib/platform/platform.test.ts (new — 18 tests); src/lib/platform/useIsMacPlatform.ts (new); src/lib/hooks/useKeyboardShortcuts.test.ts (new — 4 tests); src/components/ShortcutsModal.tsx (modified — semantic tokens + platform-aware rendering); src/components/LessonChat.tsx (modified — modLabel(isMac) hint + testid); src/app/courses/[slug]/lessons/[lessonSlug]/page.tsx (modified — TutorToolbarButton + imports); src/app/test-code-runner/page.tsx (modified — modLabel hint).","+- Quality: `npm run typecheck` clean. `npm test` 272/272 (was 248 → +24 new: 18 platform.test + 4 useKeyboardShortcuts.test + 2 fixed by toBe(false) sanity). `npm run lint` 162 errors / 24 warnings — IDENTICAL to baseline 162/24 from `git stash` of HEAD~ (no regression). Browser test (`/tmp/playwright-test-US-059.js`, 24 assertions across two contexts) passes against `/courses/gauss-basics/lessons/wprowadzenie-do-filtrowania`. Windows context (UA `Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64...`): modal `data-platform=\"non-mac\"`, modal text contains NO ⌘/⌥/⌃/⇧ glyphs, `<kbd>` labels include `Ctrl` + `Shift` (not `⌘` / `⇧`), tutor button shows `Ctrl+.`, chat hint is `Ctrl+Enter to send`, `Ctrl+B` toggles TOC (`data-collapsed false → true`), no console errors. macOS context (UA `Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X...`): modal `data-platform=\"mac\"`, modal contains both ⌘ and ⇧ glyphs, modal text does NOT contain \" Ctrl \", `<kbd>` labels include `⌘` + `⇧`, tutor button shows `⌘.`, chat hint is `⌘+Enter to send`, `Cmd+B` (Meta+B) toggles TOC, no console errors. Screenshots at /tmp/US-059-win.png and /tmp/US-059-mac.png.","+- **Learnings for future iterations:**","+  - **`isMod` was already platform-flexible** — `e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey` is the canonical \"Mod\" predicate. Anyone reading the AC (\"the keybinding handlers only listen for Cmd\") might assume the handler logic is broken and try to fix it; in fact only the display layer was wrong. The `useKeyboardShortcuts.test.ts` file added now locks this in, so a future change that \"simplifies\" `isMod` to just `e.metaKey` will break the suite.","+  - **Platform detection from `navigator.userAgent` is acceptable for shortcuts.** UA spoofing exists, but the worst case is that someone with a spoofed UA sees the \"wrong\" glyph — they still get a working modal because `isMod` accepts both modifiers. Don't reach for `navigator.platform` (deprecated), `navigator.userAgentData.platform` (Chromium-only), or feature detection — UA-string regex is the simplest correct answer here.","+  - **SSR-safe hook pattern: `useState(false) + useEffect(() => set(...))`.** Avoid `useState(() => typeof window !== 'undefined' && ...)` because Next 16's hydration phase runs the lazy initializer with `window` defined on the client but the SSR HTML was rendered with `false` — that's a hydration mismatch. Always default to the SSR value (`false` here) and flip in `useEffect`. The 1-frame flash on Mac is the price of zero hydration warnings.","+  - **Semantic tokens > literal glyphs in shortcut descriptors.** Rather than `keys: ['⌘', 'B']`, use `keys: ['mod', 'B']` and translate at render time. Future changes (a new platform, a new keymap variant for vim users, etc.) only touch `keyLabel()`, not every shortcut definition. `KeyToken` is a string literal union with a `(string & {})` fallback so literal keys like `'B'`, `'.'`, `'?'` pass through verbatim — no breaking change to call sites that mix tokens and literals.","+  - **Glyph asymmetry: `⌘.` vs `Ctrl+.`** — Mac convention is no separator (Apple menus), Windows/Linux convention is `+`. The TutorToolbarButton now matches both. Don't try to unify to `⌘+.` \"for consistency\" — it'll look wrong on Mac. The textContent test asserts the EXACT string per platform.","+  - **`↵` Enter glyph stays cross-platform.** AC only mentions ⌘/⌥/⌃ (and by inclusion ⇧) — `↵` is a universal Return symbol on both Mac and Windows keyboards. Replacing it with the word \"Enter\" would expand the `<kbd>` width unnecessarily and break vertical alignment in the modal rows. Same for arrow glyphs (`↑↓←→`), Tab, Space, Esc — these read fine on both platforms.","+  - **`page.evaluate(() => navigator.userAgent)` returns the UA the test assigned**, not the host's UA. So passing `userAgent: WINDOWS_UA` to `browser.newContext({...})` is enough to make `useIsMacPlatform()` return `false` inside the test page — no need to mock `navigator` directly. Verified: macOS context returned `data-platform=\"mac\"`, Windows context `data-platform=\"non-mac\"` from the same dev server.","+  - **TOC selector is `data-testid=\"lesson-toc\"` not `\"toc\"`.** Mistook the convention initially — every TOC-related testid is prefixed `lesson-toc-…` or `toc-<part>`. The full list is in page.tsx:1343 (root), 1399 (header), 1448 (toggle), 1496 (modules list), etc. Use `lesson-toc[data-collapsed]` for assertions about the panel's open/collapsed state.","+---"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
